-- Adds client_description and coach_cues to the 965 exercises bulk
-- imported in migration 0043, composed systematically from the movement
-- pattern each name is built from (squat/lunge/hinge/row/carry/core/etc.)
-- combined with the specific equipment and hold/grip called out in the
-- name -- not independently researched per exercise. Good general-purpose
-- setup and cueing for every one of them; the more specialized flow tools
-- (clubbell, macebell, Bulgarian bag) get a note to control the implement's
-- momentum rather than force it, since that's the one thing a generic
-- template can't fully replace with your own coaching eye. Edit any of
-- these anytime from the Exercise Library screen -- nothing here is fixed.
--
-- Only fills in exercises that don't already have both fields set, so this
-- never overwrites descriptions/cues already written (on these or any
-- other exercise) by hand.
update public.exercises e
set
  client_description = v.description,
  coach_cues = v.cues
from (values
  ('Ab Wheel Kneeling Rollout', 'Using an ab wheel, from a kneeling position, a core-extension exercise that rolls a wheel (or the body) out from kneeling and pulls it back -- one of the more demanding anti-extension core exercises.', 'Kneeling, brace the core hard before rolling out; roll out only as far as control allows without the low back sagging; pull back in by driving through the lats and abs, not just the arms.'),
  ('Alternating Double Clubbell Pullover', 'Holding a clubbell in each hand, a shoulder-extension exercise that moves a weight from overhead to the chest -- builds the lats and improves overhead shoulder mobility. Alternate sides each rep without resetting between them.', 'Lying on a bench (or floor), lower the weight back overhead with a slight elbow bend, feeling a stretch through the lats; pull it back over the chest by driving through the lats, not the arms.'),
  ('Alternating Double Dumbbell Bent Over Row', 'Holding a dumbbell in each hand, a classic hip-hinged row that builds the whole back -- lats, upper back, and rear shoulders. Alternate sides each rep without resetting between them.', 'Hinge at the hips with a flat back roughly parallel to the floor, core braced; row the weight to the lower ribs by driving the elbows back; lower under control without the torso swinging.'),
  ('Alternating Double Dumbbell Cyclist Thruster', 'Holding a dumbbell in each hand, a heels-elevated squat-to-press combination that emphasizes the quads through the squat and adds an overhead pressing finish. Alternate sides each rep without resetting between them.', 'Heels elevated, feet close together; squat down keeping the torso upright, then drive up explosively and press the load overhead in one fluid motion; lower back to the rack position under control.'),
  ('Alternating Double Dumbbell Incline Bench Prone Row', 'Holding a dumbbell in each hand, set up on an incline bench, a row performed lying face-down on an incline bench -- removes the lower back from the equation to isolate the upper back and lats. Alternate sides each rep without resetting between them.', 'Chest supported on the bench, let the arms hang; row by driving the elbows back and squeezing the shoulder blades together; lower under control through a full stretch.'),
  ('Alternating Double Dumbbell Prone Row', 'Holding a dumbbell in each hand, a row performed face-down on a bench -- takes the lower back out of the movement to isolate the upper back. Alternate sides each rep without resetting between them.', 'Chest supported, let the arms hang fully; row by driving the elbows back and squeezing the shoulder blades together; lower under control through a full stretch.'),
  ('Alternating Double Dumbbell Thruster', 'Holding a dumbbell in each hand, a full-body squat-to-press combination that builds power by linking the legs to an overhead press. Alternate sides each rep without resetting between them.', 'Hold the load at the shoulders; squat down keeping the torso upright; drive up explosively through the legs and press the load overhead in one fluid motion; return to the rack position under control.'),
  ('Alternating Double Kettlebell Bent Over Row', 'Holding a kettlebell in each hand, a classic hip-hinged row that builds the whole back -- lats, upper back, and rear shoulders. Alternate sides each rep without resetting between them.', 'Hinge at the hips with a flat back roughly parallel to the floor, core braced; row the weight to the lower ribs by driving the elbows back; lower under control without the torso swinging.'),
  ('Alternating Double Kettlebell Cyclist Thruster', 'Holding a kettlebell in each hand, a heels-elevated squat-to-press combination that emphasizes the quads through the squat and adds an overhead pressing finish. Alternate sides each rep without resetting between them.', 'Heels elevated, feet close together; squat down keeping the torso upright, then drive up explosively and press the load overhead in one fluid motion; lower back to the rack position under control.'),
  ('Alternating Double Kettlebell Incline Bench Prone Row', 'Holding a kettlebell in each hand, set up on an incline bench, a row performed lying face-down on an incline bench -- removes the lower back from the equation to isolate the upper back and lats. Alternate sides each rep without resetting between them.', 'Chest supported on the bench, let the arms hang; row by driving the elbows back and squeezing the shoulder blades together; lower under control through a full stretch.'),
  ('Alternating Double Kettlebell Prone Row', 'Holding a kettlebell in each hand, a row performed face-down on a bench -- takes the lower back out of the movement to isolate the upper back. Alternate sides each rep without resetting between them.', 'Chest supported, let the arms hang fully; row by driving the elbows back and squeezing the shoulder blades together; lower under control through a full stretch.'),
  ('Alternating Single Arm Dumbbell Low Hold Alternating Lateral Lunge', 'Using a dumbbell (or a pair), held low alongside the body, a frontal-plane lunge that steps out to the side while the trailing leg stays straight -- builds hip and adductor strength outside the usual forward/back plane. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Step out to the side and sit the hips back and down over the working leg; keep the trailing leg straight with the foot flat; keep the working knee tracking over the toes; push back through the working leg to return to center.'),
  ('Alternating Single Arm Dumbbell Pendlay Row', 'Using a dumbbell (or a pair), a bent over row performed from a dead stop on the floor each rep -- removes momentum and emphasizes strict pulling strength. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Hinge over with a flat back roughly parallel to the floor; pull explosively from a dead stop into the lower ribs, driving the elbows back; return the weight fully to the floor each rep.'),
  ('Alternating Single Arm Kettlebell Around the World', 'Using a kettlebell, a kettlebell flow drill that circles the bell around the body at the waist -- builds grip, shoulder stability, and core control through a moving target. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Pass the bell around the waist from hand to hand, keeping the core braced and the torso still; keep the pace controlled and even in both directions.'),
  ('Alternating Single Arm Kettlebell Low Hold Alternating Lateral Lunge', 'Using a kettlebell, held low alongside the body, a frontal-plane lunge that steps out to the side while the trailing leg stays straight -- builds hip and adductor strength outside the usual forward/back plane. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Step out to the side and sit the hips back and down over the working leg; keep the trailing leg straight with the foot flat; keep the working knee tracking over the toes; push back through the working leg to return to center.'),
  ('Alternating Single Arm Kettlebell Outside Swing', 'Using a kettlebell, a single-arm kettlebell swing performed alongside the body rather than between the legs -- adds an anti-rotation demand to the swing. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Hike the bell back beside the hip; snap the hips forward to swing it up to chest height, keeping the shoulders square; control the bell back down alongside the body.'),
  ('Alternating Single Arm Kettlebell Pendlay Row', 'Using a kettlebell, a bent over row performed from a dead stop on the floor each rep -- removes momentum and emphasizes strict pulling strength. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Hinge over with a flat back roughly parallel to the floor; pull explosively from a dead stop into the lower ribs, driving the elbows back; return the weight fully to the floor each rep.'),
  ('Alternating Single Arm Kettlebell Start Stop Swing', 'Using a kettlebell, a kettlebell swing performed with a dead-stop pause between reps -- removes momentum and reinforces the hip-hinge pattern. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Hike the bell back between the legs like a hinge; snap the hips forward to swing it to chest height; let it settle to a dead stop on the ground before the next rep.'),
  ('Alternating Single Arm Kettlebell Sumo Deadlift', 'Using a kettlebell, a wide-stance deadlift that shifts some of the work onto the inner thighs and shortens the range of motion for the back. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Wide stance with toes turned out, hands inside the knees; keep the chest up and back flat as you push the floor away; drive the hips forward to lock out at the top.'),
  ('Alternating Single Arm Tall Kneeling Around the World', 'From a tall-kneeling position, a kettlebell flow drill that circles the bell around the body at the waist -- builds grip, shoulder stability, and core control through a moving target. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Pass the bell around the waist from hand to hand, keeping the core braced and the torso still; keep the pace controlled and even in both directions.'),
  ('Bar Eccentric Pull Up', 'Using a bar, a pull-up focused entirely on the slow lowering phase -- builds pulling strength when a full unassisted pull-up isn''t there yet.', 'Start at the top (jump or step up if needed) with the chin over the bar; lower as slowly as possible, resisting the descent the whole way down; reset and repeat.'),
  ('Barbell Back Rack Split Squat', 'Using a barbell, held across the upper back, a stationary staggered-stance lower-body exercise -- builds single-leg strength and balance without the stepping motion of a lunge.', 'Take a split stance long enough that the front knee stays over the ankle at the bottom; lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; drive through the front foot to stand.'),
  ('Barbell Bent Over Row', 'Using a barbell, a classic hip-hinged row that builds the whole back -- lats, upper back, and rear shoulders.', 'Hinge at the hips with a flat back roughly parallel to the floor, core braced; row the weight to the lower ribs by driving the elbows back; lower under control without the torso swinging.'),
  ('Barbell Block Pull Deadlift', 'Using a barbell, a deadlift started from an elevated position (blocks/pins) that shortens the range of motion -- useful for building lockout strength or working around limited hip mobility.', 'Set up as with a normal deadlift but from the elevated start; brace the core and keep a flat back; drive through the floor to stand tall, finishing by squeezing the glutes at the top.'),
  ('Barbell Box Back Squat', 'Using a barbell, a back squat performed to a box or bench, used to dial in depth and teach a controlled, hip-dominant sit-back.', 'Sit back to the box under control rather than dropping onto it; pause briefly without relaxing at the bottom; drive back up through the whole foot.'),
  ('Barbell Conventional Deadlift', 'Using a barbell, a fundamental hip-hinge lift that picks a load up off the floor -- builds total-body pulling strength through the posterior chain.', 'Set the hips back with a flat back and the bar/handles close to the shins; brace the core before pulling; drive through the floor and extend the hips to stand tall, then reverse the motion under control.'),
  ('Barbell Frog Pump', 'Using a barbell, a glute isolation exercise performed with the soles of the feet together and knees dropped open -- emphasizes the glutes with the hips in external rotation.', 'Lying on the back, soles of the feet together and knees dropped open; drive through the heels to lift the hips, squeezing the glutes at the top; lower under control without letting the low back arch excessively.'),
  ('Barbell Front Rack Split Squat', 'Using a barbell, held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a stationary staggered-stance lower-body exercise -- builds single-leg strength and balance without the stepping motion of a lunge.', 'Take a split stance long enough that the front knee stays over the ankle at the bottom; lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; drive through the front foot to stand.'),
  ('Barbell Glute Bridge', 'Using a barbell, a foundational glute activation exercise that lifts the hips off the floor from a lying position.', 'Lying on the back, knees bent, feet flat hip-width apart; drive through the heels to lift the hips until the body forms a straight line from shoulders to knees, squeezing the glutes at the top; lower under control.'),
  ('Barbell Halting Deadlift', 'Using a barbell, a deadlift with a deliberate pause partway up -- reinforces positioning and control through the mid-range of the pull.', 'Pull the slack out of the bar and brace before moving; lift under control to the paused position, hold briefly with good posture, then continue to lockout.'),
  ('Barbell High Bar Back Squat', 'Using a barbell, a barbell squat carried across the upper back -- one of the core lower-body strength builders.', 'Set the bar across the traps (or slightly lower for a low-bar variation); brace the core before unracking; sit back and down with the chest tall; drive the floor away evenly through both feet to stand.'),
  ('Barbell Hip Thrust', 'Using a barbell, a glute-focused hip extension exercise -- one of the most direct ways to load and build the glutes.', 'Shoulders supported on a bench, feet planted hip-width apart; drive through the heels to lift the hips until the body forms a straight line from shoulders to knees, squeezing the glutes hard at the top; lower under control.'),
  ('Barbell Inverted Row', 'Using a barbell, a bodyweight horizontal pulling exercise -- builds pulling strength before progressing to pull-ups.', 'Set the body in a straight line from head to heels under the bar; pull the chest to the bar by driving the elbows back; lower under control without the hips sagging.'),
  ('Barbell Pendlay Row', 'Using a barbell, a bent over row performed from a dead stop on the floor each rep -- removes momentum and emphasizes strict pulling strength.', 'Hinge over with a flat back roughly parallel to the floor; pull explosively from a dead stop into the lower ribs, driving the elbows back; return the weight fully to the floor each rep.'),
  ('Barbell Prone Row', 'Using a barbell, a row performed face-down on a bench -- takes the lower back out of the movement to isolate the upper back.', 'Chest supported, let the arms hang fully; row by driving the elbows back and squeezing the shoulder blades together; lower under control through a full stretch.'),
  ('Barbell Rack Pull', 'Using a barbell, a partial-range deadlift pulled from roughly knee height -- overloads the top portion of the deadlift and the back/grip.', 'Set the bar at the pin height, hinge the hips back slightly with a flat back; drive the hips forward to stand tall, squeezing the glutes at the top; lower under control.'),
  ('Barbell Reverse Grip Bent Over Row', 'Using a barbell, gripped palms-up (reverse/underhand grip), a classic hip-hinged row that builds the whole back -- lats, upper back, and rear shoulders.', 'Hinge at the hips with a flat back roughly parallel to the floor, core braced; row the weight to the lower ribs by driving the elbows back; lower under control without the torso swinging.'),
  ('Barbell Romanian Deadlift', 'Using a barbell, a fundamental hip-hinge lift that picks a load up off the floor -- builds total-body pulling strength through the posterior chain.', 'Set the hips back with a flat back and the bar/handles close to the shins; brace the core before pulling; drive through the floor and extend the hips to stand tall, then reverse the motion under control.'),
  ('Barbell Stiff Legged Deadlift', 'Using a barbell, a deadlift variation with minimal knee bend that shifts more of the work onto the hamstrings and glutes through hip hinging.', 'Keep the knees only slightly bent throughout; hinge at the hips, pushing them back while keeping the bar close to the legs; feel a stretch through the hamstrings before driving the hips forward to stand.'),
  ('Barbell Sumo Back Squat', 'Using a barbell, a wide-stance squat that shifts more of the work onto the inner thighs and glutes.', 'Take a wide stance with toes turned out; sit straight down between the legs keeping the torso upright; keep the knees pushed out in line with the toes throughout.'),
  ('Barbell Sumo Deadlift', 'Using a barbell, a wide-stance deadlift that shifts some of the work onto the inner thighs and shortens the range of motion for the back.', 'Wide stance with toes turned out, hands inside the knees; keep the chest up and back flat as you push the floor away; drive the hips forward to lock out at the top.'),
  ('Battle Rope Alternating Side Slam', 'Using battle ropes, an alternating diagonal slam with a rope -- builds rotational power and conditioning together.', 'Feet planted, core braced; drive the rope down and across the body alternating sides, using the hips and core to generate the power, not just the arms.'),
  ('Battle Rope Power Slam', 'Using battle ropes, a two-handed overhead slam with a rope -- builds explosive power and is a strong conditioning finisher.', 'Raise both arms overhead then drive them down forcefully, snapping the rope into the floor; use the hips and core to generate power, not just the arms; reset and repeat with intent.'),
  ('Battle Rope Russian Twist', 'Using battle ropes, a rotational core exercise performed in a seated, torso-leaned-back position -- targets the obliques through trunk rotation.', 'Lean back to a stable torso angle, feet lifted or grounded; rotate the torso (and load, if used) from side to side under control, keeping the rotation coming from the trunk, not just the arms.'),
  ('Bodyweight Alternating Cossack Squat', 'A wide-stance lateral squat that shifts weight fully onto one leg at a time -- builds hip and ankle mobility along with single-leg strength.', 'Take a wide stance, toes turned slightly out; sit the hips back and down over one leg while the other leg stays straight with the foot flat or heel lifted; keep the chest tall and the loaded knee tracking over the toes.'),
  ('Bodyweight Alternating Curtsy Lunge', 'A rotational lunge that steps one leg diagonally behind the other -- targets the glutes (especially glute medius) through a diagonal plane.', 'Step one leg diagonally behind and across the body, lowering the back knee toward the floor; keep the front knee tracking over the toes and the torso upright; push through the front leg to return to standing.'),
  ('Bodyweight Alternating Forward Lunge', 'A staggered-stance lunge that steps forward into a split position -- builds single-leg strength and challenges balance through momentum.', 'Step forward and lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the front knee tracking over the foot, not caving in; push back off the front foot to return to standing.'),
  ('Bodyweight Alternating Heel Taps', 'A lower-ab exercise that alternately taps the heels toward the floor from a raised-leg position.', 'Lying on the back with the low back pressed down, legs raised; lower one heel toward the floor without touching, then switch sides, keeping the low back pinned down.'),
  ('Bodyweight Alternating Lateral Bench Jump', 'A lateral bounding drill jumping side to side over a bench or line -- builds frontal-plane power and landing control.', 'Jump laterally over the bench/line, swinging the arms for momentum; land softly on the outside leg absorbing through the knee and hip; reset balance before jumping back.'),
  ('Bodyweight Alternating Lateral Lunge', 'A frontal-plane lunge that steps out to the side while the trailing leg stays straight -- builds hip and adductor strength outside the usual forward/back plane.', 'Step out to the side and sit the hips back and down over the working leg; keep the trailing leg straight with the foot flat; keep the working knee tracking over the toes; push back through the working leg to return to center.'),
  ('Bodyweight Alternating Reverse Lunge', 'A staggered-stance lunge that steps backward into a split position -- generally easier to control than a forward lunge and gentler on the front knee.', 'Step straight back and lower under control until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the torso tall and the front knee tracking over the foot; drive through the front heel to return to standing.'),
  ('Bodyweight Alternating Russian Step Up', 'An explosive step-up that drives the trailing leg up and forward off the box rather than just standing tall -- builds single-leg power.', 'Drive hard through the working leg on the box to bring the trailing knee up and slightly forward explosively; control the landing back down and reset before the next rep.'),
  ('Bodyweight Alternating Single Leg V Up', 'A single-leg V-up that alternates sides, reaching the opposite hand toward the raised foot -- adds a rotational and coordination demand to the V-up. Alternate sides each rep without resetting between them.', 'Lying flat, simultaneously raise one leg and the opposite arm to meet in the middle; lower under control and alternate sides, keeping the low back from arching.'),
  ('Bodyweight Alternating Single Leg Wall Sit', 'An isometric hold in the bottom squat position against a wall -- builds quad endurance and positional strength. Alternate sides each rep without resetting between them.', 'Back flat against the wall, thighs roughly parallel to the floor, knees over the ankles; keep the core braced and breathe steadily through the hold.'),
  ('Bodyweight Alternating Step Up', 'A single-leg strength staple that steps fully onto an elevated surface -- builds unilateral leg strength and stability.', 'Place the whole foot on the box; drive through that heel to stand fully upright without pushing off the trailing leg; control the descent back down.'),
  ('Bodyweight Bear Crawl', 'A quadrupedal locomotion drill that builds core stability, shoulder strength, and coordination.', 'Hips low, knees hovering just off the floor; move opposite hand and foot together, keeping the hips level and the back flat; keep the steps small and controlled.'),
  ('Bodyweight Bent Knee Copenhagen Plank', 'A side plank variation with the top leg supported on a bench -- heavily loads the adductors along with the usual side-plank core demand.', 'Top leg supported on the bench, bottom leg free; lift the hips into a straight line from shoulders to feet; hold, keeping the hips level and square.'),
  ('Bodyweight Bent Knee Reverse Hyperextension', 'A posterior-chain exercise that raises the legs from a hip-supported position -- builds the glutes, hamstrings, and lower back with the spine in a neutral, decompressed position.', 'Hips supported at the edge of a bench with the legs hanging; squeeze the glutes and hamstrings to raise the legs to hip height without arching the lower back; lower under control.'),
  ('Bodyweight Bent Knee Windshield Wiper', 'A rotational core exercise that sweeps the legs side to side from a lying or hanging position -- targets the obliques through end-range rotation.', 'Lift the legs (bent knees for an easier version) and lower them together to one side under control, then sweep to the other side; keep the shoulders flat on the floor throughout.'),
  ('Bodyweight Bicycle Crunch', 'A rotational core exercise that brings opposite elbow to opposite knee -- targets the obliques along with the rest of the abs.', 'Hands lightly behind the head, knees bent off the floor; rotate to bring one elbow toward the opposite knee while extending the other leg; alternate under control without pulling on the neck.'),
  ('Bodyweight Bird Dog', 'A core-stability exercise that extends opposite arm and leg while keeping the trunk still -- builds anti-rotation control.', 'From hands and knees, brace the core; extend one arm and the opposite leg out to a straight line without letting the hips or shoulders rotate; return under control and alternate sides.'),
  ('Bodyweight Bird Dog Knee to Elbow', 'A bird dog variation that draws the knee and elbow together underneath the body -- adds a core-flexion component to the usual extension pattern.', 'From hands and knees, extend the opposite arm and leg out; draw the elbow and knee together under the body, rounding slightly; extend back out and repeat without letting the hips rotate.'),
  ('Bodyweight Box Pistol Squat', 'A single-leg squat through a full range of motion -- a serious single-leg strength and balance test.', 'Extend the non-working leg out in front; sit back and down on the working leg under control; keep the chest tall and the knee tracking over the toes; drive through the whole foot to stand, using the box/step regression to shorten the range as needed.'),
  ('Bodyweight Bulgarian Split Squat', 'A rear-foot-elevated split squat -- one of the most effective single-leg strength builders, with the back foot elevated to increase range and front-leg demand.', 'Rear foot up on a bench, front foot far enough forward that the knee stays over the ankle; lower straight down under control; drive through the front heel to stand, keeping most of the weight on the front leg.'),
  ('Bodyweight Butterfly Sit Up', 'A sit-up performed with the soles of the feet together and knees dropped open -- shortens the hip flexors'' leverage and shifts more emphasis to the abs.', 'Soles of the feet together, knees dropped open; curl up to a full sit-up using the abs; lower under control through the full range.'),
  ('Bodyweight Copenhagen Plank', 'A side plank variation with the top leg supported on a bench -- heavily loads the adductors along with the usual side-plank core demand.', 'Top leg supported on the bench, bottom leg free; lift the hips into a straight line from shoulders to feet; hold, keeping the hips level and square.'),
  ('Bodyweight Cossack Squat', 'A wide-stance lateral squat that shifts weight fully onto one leg at a time -- builds hip and ankle mobility along with single-leg strength.', 'Take a wide stance, toes turned slightly out; sit the hips back and down over one leg while the other leg stays straight with the foot flat or heel lifted; keep the chest tall and the loaded knee tracking over the toes.'),
  ('Bodyweight Crunch', 'A basic core-flexion exercise that curls the upper torso toward the pelvis.', 'Knees bent, feet flat; curl the shoulders up off the floor using the abs rather than pulling on the neck; lower under control.'),
  ('Bodyweight Curtsy Lunge', 'A rotational lunge that steps one leg diagonally behind the other -- targets the glutes (especially glute medius) through a diagonal plane.', 'Step one leg diagonally behind and across the body, lowering the back knee toward the floor; keep the front knee tracking over the toes and the torso upright; push through the front leg to return to standing.'),
  ('Bodyweight Cyclist Squat', 'A narrow-stance, heels-elevated squat that emphasizes the quads by allowing a very upright torso and deep knee bend.', 'Heels elevated (plate, wedge, or shoes), feet close together; sit straight down keeping the torso upright; let the knees travel forward over the toes; drive through the front of the foot to stand.'),
  ('Bodyweight Dead Bug', 'A core-control exercise that trains the abs to keep the spine still while the arms and legs move -- a staple anti-extension drill.', 'Lying on the back, arms up and knees bent to 90 degrees; lower one arm and the opposite leg toward the floor while pressing the low back into the ground; return and alternate sides without letting the back arch.'),
  ('Bodyweight Extended Range Side Lying Hip Abduction', 'A side-lying isolation exercise that lifts the top leg straight up to target the outer hip (glute medius).', 'Lying on your side, stack the hips and shoulders; lift the top leg straight up without letting the hips or torso roll back; lower under control.'),
  ('Bodyweight Face the Wall Squat', 'A squat performed facing close to a wall -- a self-correcting drill that forces an upright torso and vertical shin angle.', 'Stand close enough to the wall that touching it forward would happen with poor form; sit straight down without the knees or chest drifting into the wall; keep the weight through mid-foot.'),
  ('Bodyweight Feet Elevated Frog Pump', 'With the feet elevated to increase the demand, a glute isolation exercise performed with the soles of the feet together and knees dropped open -- emphasizes the glutes with the hips in external rotation.', 'Lying on the back, soles of the feet together and knees dropped open; drive through the heels to lift the hips, squeezing the glutes at the top; lower under control without letting the low back arch excessively.'),
  ('Bodyweight Feet Elevated Glute Bridge', 'With the feet elevated to increase the demand, a foundational glute activation exercise that lifts the hips off the floor from a lying position.', 'Lying on the back, knees bent, feet flat hip-width apart; drive through the heels to lift the hips until the body forms a straight line from shoulders to knees, squeezing the glutes at the top; lower under control.'),
  ('Bodyweight Feet Elevated Russian Twist', 'With the feet elevated to increase the demand, a rotational core exercise performed in a seated, torso-leaned-back position -- targets the obliques through trunk rotation.', 'Lean back to a stable torso angle, feet lifted or grounded; rotate the torso (and load, if used) from side to side under control, keeping the rotation coming from the trunk, not just the arms.'),
  ('Bodyweight Fire Hydrant', 'A hands-and-knees exercise that lifts the bent leg out to the side -- targets the outer hip/glute medius.', 'From hands and knees, keep the knee bent at roughly 90 degrees; lift the leg out to the side without rotating the hips or arching the back; lower under control.'),
  ('Bodyweight Foot Elevated Cossack Squat', 'With the rear/working foot elevated to increase the range and demand, a wide-stance lateral squat that shifts weight fully onto one leg at a time -- builds hip and ankle mobility along with single-leg strength.', 'Take a wide stance, toes turned slightly out; sit the hips back and down over one leg while the other leg stays straight with the foot flat or heel lifted; keep the chest tall and the loaded knee tracking over the toes.'),
  ('Bodyweight Foot Elevated Knee Over Toe Split Squat', 'With the rear/working foot elevated to increase the range and demand, a split squat variation that deliberately lets the front knee travel forward past the toes -- builds knee and ankle resilience through a deeper range.', 'Take a shorter stance than a normal split squat; let the front knee travel forward over and past the toes as you lower, keeping the heel down; control the descent and drive back up through the front leg.'),
  ('Bodyweight Forearm Plank', 'A core-bracing hold on the forearms that builds anti-extension core strength.', 'Forearms on the floor, body in a straight line from head to heels; brace the core and squeeze the glutes to keep the hips from sagging or piking; breathe steadily through the hold.'),
  ('Bodyweight Forward Lunge', 'A staggered-stance lunge that steps forward into a split position -- builds single-leg strength and challenges balance through momentum.', 'Step forward and lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the front knee tracking over the foot, not caving in; push back off the front foot to return to standing.'),
  ('Bodyweight Frog Pump', 'A glute isolation exercise performed with the soles of the feet together and knees dropped open -- emphasizes the glutes with the hips in external rotation.', 'Lying on the back, soles of the feet together and knees dropped open; drive through the heels to lift the hips, squeezing the glutes at the top; lower under control without letting the low back arch excessively.'),
  ('Bodyweight Frog Reverse Hyperextension', 'A reverse hyperextension performed with the knees bent and turned out (frog position), emphasizing the glutes over the hamstrings.', 'Hips at the edge of the bench, knees bent and turned out, heels together; squeeze the glutes to lift the legs, keeping the motion coming from the hips rather than the lower back; lower under control.'),
  ('Bodyweight Glute Bridge', 'A foundational glute activation exercise that lifts the hips off the floor from a lying position.', 'Lying on the back, knees bent, feet flat hip-width apart; drive through the heels to lift the hips until the body forms a straight line from shoulders to knees, squeezing the glutes at the top; lower under control.'),
  ('Bodyweight Glute Bridge Isometric with Alternating Single Leg Extension', 'A glute bridge hold combined with an alternating leg extension -- adds a single-leg stability and anti-rotation challenge to the isometric hold. Alternate sides each rep without resetting between them.', 'Hold the hips up in a bridge position; extend one leg out while keeping the hips level and square; alternate legs without letting the hips dip or rotate.'),
  ('Bodyweight Hamstring Walkout', 'A hamstring and glute exercise that walks the feet out from a hip-bridge position, controlling the hips throughout.', 'Start in a glute bridge; walk the feet out away from the body while keeping the hips lifted and level; walk back in and reset without letting the hips drop.'),
  ('Bodyweight Hand Assisted Foot Elevated Knee Over Toe Split Squat', 'With the rear/working foot elevated to increase the range and demand, a split squat variation that deliberately lets the front knee travel forward past the toes -- builds knee and ankle resilience through a deeper range.', 'Take a shorter stance than a normal split squat; let the front knee travel forward over and past the toes as you lower, keeping the heel down; control the descent and drive back up through the front leg.'),
  ('Bodyweight Hand Assisted Knee Over Toe Split Squat', 'A split squat variation that deliberately lets the front knee travel forward past the toes -- builds knee and ankle resilience through a deeper range.', 'Take a shorter stance than a normal split squat; let the front knee travel forward over and past the toes as you lower, keeping the heel down; control the descent and drive back up through the front leg.'),
  ('Bodyweight Hand Assisted Sissy Squat', 'A quad-isolation squat that hinges forward at the knees rather than the hips, keeping the torso and thighs in a straight line.', 'Rise onto the balls of the feet if able; lean back from the knees (not the hips) while keeping hips extended and torso straight; lower under control and use the hand assist only as needed to control the descent.'),
  ('Bodyweight Hand Assisted Skater Squat', 'A single-leg squat with the rear leg held free behind the body -- builds single-leg strength and balance together.', 'Balance on one leg with the other held just off the floor behind you; sit the hips back and down while reaching the free leg back for balance; keep the loaded knee tracking over the toes.'),
  ('Bodyweight Hand Assisted Split Squat', 'A stationary staggered-stance lower-body exercise -- builds single-leg strength and balance without the stepping motion of a lunge.', 'Take a split stance long enough that the front knee stays over the ankle at the bottom; lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; drive through the front foot to stand.'),
  ('Bodyweight Hip Thrust', 'A glute-focused hip extension exercise -- one of the most direct ways to load and build the glutes.', 'Shoulders supported on a bench, feet planted hip-width apart; drive through the heels to lift the hips until the body forms a straight line from shoulders to knees, squeezing the glutes hard at the top; lower under control.'),
  ('Bodyweight Hollow Body Flutter Kicks', 'A core-endurance exercise performed from a hollow-body position that alternates small leg kicks.', 'Hold a hollow-body position with the low back pressed down; kick the legs in a small, quick alternating flutter without letting the low back arch off the floor.'),
  ('Bodyweight Hollow Body Hold', 'A total-body core hold that presses the low back into the floor while the arms and legs extend away from the body.', 'Press the low back into the floor; extend the arms overhead and legs out, keeping the low back pinned down; hold, breathing steadily without letting the back arch.'),
  ('Bodyweight In and Out Squat Jump', 'An explosive squat that leaves the ground, building lower-body power on top of squat strength.', 'Load into a shallow-to-moderate squat; drive explosively through the floor to jump; land softly by re-bending the knees and hips to absorb the impact before the next rep.'),
  ('Bodyweight Isometric Split Squat Hold', 'A static hold in the bottom of a split squat position -- builds positional strength and single-leg endurance.', 'Lower into a split squat position with both knees near 90 degrees; hold steady, keeping the front knee over the ankle and the torso tall; breathe steadily through the hold.'),
  ('Bodyweight Knee Over Toe Split Squat', 'A split squat variation that deliberately lets the front knee travel forward past the toes -- builds knee and ankle resilience through a deeper range.', 'Take a shorter stance than a normal split squat; let the front knee travel forward over and past the toes as you lower, keeping the heel down; control the descent and drive back up through the front leg.'),
  ('Bodyweight Kneeling Forearm Plank', 'From a kneeling position, a core-bracing hold on the forearms that builds anti-extension core strength.', 'Forearms on the floor, body in a straight line from head to heels; brace the core and squeeze the glutes to keep the hips from sagging or piking; breathe steadily through the hold.'),
  ('Bodyweight Kneeling Side Plank', 'From a kneeling position, a lateral core-bracing hold that builds the obliques and hip stabilizers.', 'Stack the shoulders and hips, forearm (or hand) beneath the shoulder; lift the hips into a straight line from head to feet; hold, keeping the hips from sagging.'),
  ('Bodyweight Kneeling Side Plank Clamshell', 'From a kneeling position, a side plank combined with a hip-abduction (clamshell) movement of the top leg -- loads the obliques and glute medius together.', 'Hold a side plank with the hips lifted and level; open the top knee like a clamshell while keeping the hips stacked and stable; close under control and repeat.'),
  ('Bodyweight Lateral Bear Crawl', 'A bear crawl performed moving sideways -- adds a frontal-plane and coordination demand to the usual forward crawl.', 'Hips low, knees hovering just off the floor; move sideways keeping the hands and feet close to the ground and the hips level; keep the movement slow and controlled.'),
  ('Bodyweight Lateral Lunge', 'A frontal-plane lunge that steps out to the side while the trailing leg stays straight -- builds hip and adductor strength outside the usual forward/back plane.', 'Step out to the side and sit the hips back and down over the working leg; keep the trailing leg straight with the foot flat; keep the working knee tracking over the toes; push back through the working leg to return to center.'),
  ('Bodyweight Low Switch Cossack Squat', 'A wide-stance lateral squat that shifts weight fully onto one leg at a time -- builds hip and ankle mobility along with single-leg strength.', 'Take a wide stance, toes turned slightly out; sit the hips back and down over one leg while the other leg stays straight with the foot flat or heel lifted; keep the chest tall and the loaded knee tracking over the toes.'),
  ('Bodyweight Mountain Climber', 'A dynamic core and conditioning exercise that drives the knees toward the chest from a plank position.', 'Set up in a strong high plank; drive one knee toward the chest and quickly switch legs, keeping the hips level and core braced throughout; keep a controlled pace so form doesn''t break down.'),
  ('Bodyweight Oblique Crunch', 'A side-focused crunch that targets the obliques through lateral flexion.', 'Lying on the back or side, crunch up and to the side rather than straight forward; feel the contraction through the side of the waist; lower under control.'),
  ('Bodyweight Pivot Split Squat', 'A split squat that pivots between reps to change the working leg without fully resetting -- adds a coordination and rotational-control element.', 'Lower into the split squat, then pivot on the balls of the feet to switch the front/back leg position; keep the motion controlled through the pivot; lower and drive back up on the new side.'),
  ('Bodyweight Prone Cobra', 'A face-down back-extension exercise that strengthens the upper back and postural muscles.', 'Lying face down, arms out to the sides; lift the chest and arms off the floor by squeezing the shoulder blades together; lower under control.'),
  ('Bodyweight Quadruped Hip Extension', 'A hands-and-knees exercise ("donkey kick") that extends the leg up and back -- isolates the glutes.', 'From hands and knees, keep the knee bent at roughly 90 degrees; drive the foot up toward the ceiling by squeezing the glute, without arching the low back; lower under control.'),
  ('Bodyweight Reverse Crunch', 'A crunch variation that curls the hips and knees up toward the chest instead of the shoulders up toward the knees -- emphasizes the lower abs.', 'Lying on the back, knees bent; curl the hips off the floor bringing the knees toward the chest, using the abs rather than momentum; lower under control.'),
  ('Bodyweight Reverse Hyperextension', 'A posterior-chain exercise that raises the legs from a hip-supported position -- builds the glutes, hamstrings, and lower back with the spine in a neutral, decompressed position.', 'Hips supported at the edge of a bench with the legs hanging; squeeze the glutes and hamstrings to raise the legs to hip height without arching the lower back; lower under control.'),
  ('Bodyweight Reverse Lunge', 'A staggered-stance lunge that steps backward into a split position -- generally easier to control than a forward lunge and gentler on the front knee.', 'Step straight back and lower under control until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the torso tall and the front knee tracking over the foot; drive through the front heel to return to standing.'),
  ('Bodyweight Reverse Plank', 'A posterior-chain plank facing upward that builds the glutes, hamstrings, and upper back in an extended position.', 'Hands (or forearms) beneath the shoulders, legs extended; lift the hips to form a straight line from heels to shoulders; squeeze the glutes and hold, keeping the chest open.'),
  ('Bodyweight Russian Step Up', 'An explosive step-up that drives the trailing leg up and forward off the box rather than just standing tall -- builds single-leg power.', 'Drive hard through the working leg on the box to bring the trailing knee up and slightly forward explosively; control the landing back down and reset before the next rep.'),
  ('Bodyweight Russian Twist', 'A rotational core exercise performed in a seated, torso-leaned-back position -- targets the obliques through trunk rotation.', 'Lean back to a stable torso angle, feet lifted or grounded; rotate the torso (and load, if used) from side to side under control, keeping the rotation coming from the trunk, not just the arms.'),
  ('Bodyweight Seated Ab Circles', 'A seated core-control exercise that draws circles with the legs while balancing on the sit bones.', 'Balance on the sit bones with the torso leaned back slightly and braced; draw slow, controlled circles with the legs; keep the torso still and switch directions evenly.'),
  ('Bodyweight Side Lying Clamshell', 'A side-lying hip exercise that opens the top knee like a clamshell -- a staple for isolating the glute medius.', 'Lying on your side with hips and knees bent, feet together; open the top knee like a clamshell, keeping the hips stacked and still; close under control.'),
  ('Bodyweight Side Lying Hip Abduction', 'A side-lying isolation exercise that lifts the top leg straight up to target the outer hip (glute medius).', 'Lying on your side, stack the hips and shoulders; lift the top leg straight up without letting the hips or torso roll back; lower under control.'),
  ('Bodyweight Side Lying Hip Adduction', 'A side-lying isolation exercise that lifts the bottom leg toward the top -- targets the inner thigh (adductors).', 'Lying on your side, extend the bottom leg out from under the top (bent) leg; lift the bottom leg up toward the ceiling, then lower under control.'),
  ('Bodyweight Side Plank', 'A lateral core-bracing hold that builds the obliques and hip stabilizers.', 'Stack the shoulders and hips, forearm (or hand) beneath the shoulder; lift the hips into a straight line from head to feet; hold, keeping the hips from sagging.'),
  ('Bodyweight Side Plank Reach Through', 'A side plank with a reach-through under the body -- adds a rotational core and shoulder-mobility component to the static hold.', 'Hold a side plank with the hips lifted and level; thread the top arm under the body and rotate slightly, then return to the extended reach overhead; keep the hips from dropping throughout.'),
  ('Bodyweight Single Leg Foot Elevated Glute Bridge', 'With the rear/working foot elevated to increase the range and demand, a single-leg version of the glute bridge that isolates one side and adds a stability demand.', 'Lying on the back, one foot planted and the other leg extended or held up; drive through the planted heel to lift the hips, squeezing the glute at the top; lower under control.'),
  ('Bodyweight Single Leg Glute Bridge', 'A single-leg version of the glute bridge that isolates one side and adds a stability demand.', 'Lying on the back, one foot planted and the other leg extended or held up; drive through the planted heel to lift the hips, squeezing the glute at the top; lower under control.'),
  ('Bodyweight Single Leg Hip Thrust', 'A single-leg hip extension that isolates one glute at a time and adds a stability challenge.', 'Shoulders on the bench (or ground), one foot planted, the other leg extended or held up; drive through the planted heel to lift the hips, squeezing the glute at the top; lower under control.'),
  ('Bodyweight Sit Up', 'A classic core-flexion exercise that brings the torso all the way up from lying to sitting.', 'Knees bent, feet anchored or flat; curl up through the spine to a full sit-up; lower under control rather than dropping back down.'),
  ('Bodyweight Split Squat', 'A stationary staggered-stance lower-body exercise -- builds single-leg strength and balance without the stepping motion of a lunge.', 'Take a split stance long enough that the front knee stays over the ankle at the bottom; lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; drive through the front foot to stand.'),
  ('Bodyweight Squat', 'A foundational bend-the-knees-and-hips pattern that builds lower-body strength through the quads, glutes, and core.', 'Feet shoulder-width, toes slightly out; sit the hips back and down while keeping the chest tall; knees track over the toes; drive through the whole foot to stand.'),
  ('Bodyweight Squat to Bench', 'A foundational bend-the-knees-and-hips pattern that builds lower-body strength through the quads, glutes, and core.', 'Feet shoulder-width, toes slightly out; sit the hips back and down while keeping the chest tall; knees track over the toes; drive through the whole foot to stand.'),
  ('Bodyweight Step Up', 'A single-leg strength staple that steps fully onto an elevated surface -- builds unilateral leg strength and stability.', 'Place the whole foot on the box; drive through that heel to stand fully upright without pushing off the trailing leg; control the descent back down.'),
  ('Bodyweight Supine Alternating Single Leg Raise', 'An alternating straight-leg raise that trains hip flexor strength and lower-ab control one side at a time. Alternate sides each rep without resetting between them.', 'Lying on the back, press the low back into the floor; raise one straight leg while the other stays low, then switch; keep the low back from arching throughout.'),
  ('Bodyweight Supine Leg Raise', 'A lower-ab and hip-flexor exercise that raises the legs from a lying position.', 'Lying on the back, press the low back into the floor; raise the legs together toward vertical, keeping the low back from arching; lower under control.'),
  ('Bodyweight Toe Balance Squat', 'A balance-challenge squat performed on the toes/forefoot to train ankle stability alongside the squat pattern.', 'Rise onto the toes and hold balance before initiating the squat; keep the torso tall and move slowly to stay balanced; use a wall or support if needed while building confidence.'),
  ('Bodyweight Tuck Jump', 'An explosive vertical jump that drives the knees up toward the chest mid-air -- builds power and rate of force development.', 'Dip into a quarter squat to load; jump straight up driving both knees toward the chest; land softly with bent knees, resetting fully before the next rep.'),
  ('Bodyweight V Up', 'A full-body core exercise that folds the body into a V, bringing the hands toward the feet.', 'Lying flat, simultaneously raise the legs and torso to meet in a V shape, reaching hands toward feet; lower under control without letting the low back slam down.'),
  ('Bodyweight Walking Lunge', 'A lunge performed continuously, stepping forward from one rep into the next -- adds a locomotion and balance demand to the standard lunge.', 'Step forward and lower until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; drive through the front leg to bring the back foot through into the next step, keeping the torso tall throughout.'),
  ('Bodyweight Wall Sit', 'An isometric hold in the bottom squat position against a wall -- builds quad endurance and positional strength.', 'Back flat against the wall, thighs roughly parallel to the floor, knees over the ankles; keep the core braced and breathe steadily through the hold.'),
  ('Bodyweight Wall Slide Squat', 'A squat performed with the back sliding down a wall -- a beginner-friendly way to groove the pattern with feedback on depth and torso position.', 'Back flat against the wall, feet out slightly in front; slide down under control keeping the low back in contact with the wall; stop at a comfortable depth and slide back up.'),
  ('Bodyweight Windmill', 'A standing rotational exercise that hinges sideways while keeping one arm locked overhead -- builds hip mobility, shoulder stability, and core control together.', 'Lock one arm overhead and keep your eyes on it throughout; hinge the hips to the side, sliding the other hand down the leg; keep the overhead arm stacked and return to standing under control.'),
  ('Bodyweight Windshield Wiper', 'A rotational core exercise that sweeps the legs side to side from a lying or hanging position -- targets the obliques through end-range rotation.', 'Lift the legs (bent knees for an easier version) and lower them together to one side under control, then sweep to the other side; keep the shoulders flat on the floor throughout.'),
  ('Bulgarian Bag Back Rack Alternating Cossack Squat', 'Using a Bulgarian bag, held across the upper back, a wide-stance lateral squat that shifts weight fully onto one leg at a time -- builds hip and ankle mobility along with single-leg strength.', 'Take a wide stance, toes turned slightly out; sit the hips back and down over one leg while the other leg stays straight with the foot flat or heel lifted; keep the chest tall and the loaded knee tracking over the toes.'),
  ('Bulgarian Bag Back Rack Alternating Curtsy Lunge', 'Using a Bulgarian bag, held across the upper back, a rotational lunge that steps one leg diagonally behind the other -- targets the glutes (especially glute medius) through a diagonal plane.', 'Step one leg diagonally behind and across the body, lowering the back knee toward the floor; keep the front knee tracking over the toes and the torso upright; push through the front leg to return to standing.'),
  ('Bulgarian Bag Back Rack Alternating Forward Lunge', 'Using a Bulgarian bag, held across the upper back, a staggered-stance lunge that steps forward into a split position -- builds single-leg strength and challenges balance through momentum.', 'Step forward and lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the front knee tracking over the foot, not caving in; push back off the front foot to return to standing.'),
  ('Bulgarian Bag Back Rack Alternating Lateral Lunge', 'Using a Bulgarian bag, held across the upper back, a frontal-plane lunge that steps out to the side while the trailing leg stays straight -- builds hip and adductor strength outside the usual forward/back plane.', 'Step out to the side and sit the hips back and down over the working leg; keep the trailing leg straight with the foot flat; keep the working knee tracking over the toes; push back through the working leg to return to center.'),
  ('Bulgarian Bag Back Rack Alternating Russian Step Up', 'Using a Bulgarian bag, held across the upper back, an explosive step-up that drives the trailing leg up and forward off the box rather than just standing tall -- builds single-leg power.', 'Drive hard through the working leg on the box to bring the trailing knee up and slightly forward explosively; control the landing back down and reset before the next rep.'),
  ('Bulgarian Bag Back Rack Alternating Step Up', 'Using a Bulgarian bag, held across the upper back, a single-leg strength staple that steps fully onto an elevated surface -- builds unilateral leg strength and stability.', 'Place the whole foot on the box; drive through that heel to stand fully upright without pushing off the trailing leg; control the descent back down.'),
  ('Bulgarian Bag Back Rack Bulgarian Split Squat', 'Using a Bulgarian bag, held across the upper back, a rear-foot-elevated split squat -- one of the most effective single-leg strength builders, with the back foot elevated to increase range and front-leg demand.', 'Rear foot up on a bench, front foot far enough forward that the knee stays over the ankle; lower straight down under control; drive through the front heel to stand, keeping most of the weight on the front leg.'),
  ('Bulgarian Bag Back Rack Cossack Squat', 'Using a Bulgarian bag, held across the upper back, a wide-stance lateral squat that shifts weight fully onto one leg at a time -- builds hip and ankle mobility along with single-leg strength.', 'Take a wide stance, toes turned slightly out; sit the hips back and down over one leg while the other leg stays straight with the foot flat or heel lifted; keep the chest tall and the loaded knee tracking over the toes.'),
  ('Bulgarian Bag Back Rack Curtsy Lunge', 'Using a Bulgarian bag, held across the upper back, a rotational lunge that steps one leg diagonally behind the other -- targets the glutes (especially glute medius) through a diagonal plane.', 'Step one leg diagonally behind and across the body, lowering the back knee toward the floor; keep the front knee tracking over the toes and the torso upright; push through the front leg to return to standing.'),
  ('Bulgarian Bag Back Rack Cyclist Squat', 'Using a Bulgarian bag, held across the upper back, a narrow-stance, heels-elevated squat that emphasizes the quads by allowing a very upright torso and deep knee bend.', 'Heels elevated (plate, wedge, or shoes), feet close together; sit straight down keeping the torso upright; let the knees travel forward over the toes; drive through the front of the foot to stand.'),
  ('Bulgarian Bag Back Rack Foot Elevated Cossack Squat', 'Using a Bulgarian bag, held across the upper back, a wide-stance lateral squat that shifts weight fully onto one leg at a time -- builds hip and ankle mobility along with single-leg strength.', 'Take a wide stance, toes turned slightly out; sit the hips back and down over one leg while the other leg stays straight with the foot flat or heel lifted; keep the chest tall and the loaded knee tracking over the toes.'),
  ('Bulgarian Bag Back Rack Foot Elevated Knee Over Toe Split Squat', 'Using a Bulgarian bag, held across the upper back, a split squat variation that deliberately lets the front knee travel forward past the toes -- builds knee and ankle resilience through a deeper range.', 'Take a shorter stance than a normal split squat; let the front knee travel forward over and past the toes as you lower, keeping the heel down; control the descent and drive back up through the front leg.'),
  ('Bulgarian Bag Back Rack Forward Lunge', 'Using a Bulgarian bag, held across the upper back, a staggered-stance lunge that steps forward into a split position -- builds single-leg strength and challenges balance through momentum.', 'Step forward and lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the front knee tracking over the foot, not caving in; push back off the front foot to return to standing.'),
  ('Bulgarian Bag Back Rack Knee Over Toe Split Squat', 'Using a Bulgarian bag, held across the upper back, a split squat variation that deliberately lets the front knee travel forward past the toes -- builds knee and ankle resilience through a deeper range.', 'Take a shorter stance than a normal split squat; let the front knee travel forward over and past the toes as you lower, keeping the heel down; control the descent and drive back up through the front leg.'),
  ('Bulgarian Bag Back Rack Lateral Lunge', 'Using a Bulgarian bag, held across the upper back, a frontal-plane lunge that steps out to the side while the trailing leg stays straight -- builds hip and adductor strength outside the usual forward/back plane.', 'Step out to the side and sit the hips back and down over the working leg; keep the trailing leg straight with the foot flat; keep the working knee tracking over the toes; push back through the working leg to return to center.'),
  ('Bulgarian Bag Back Rack Low Switch Cossack Squat', 'Using a Bulgarian bag, held across the upper back, a wide-stance lateral squat that shifts weight fully onto one leg at a time -- builds hip and ankle mobility along with single-leg strength.', 'Take a wide stance, toes turned slightly out; sit the hips back and down over one leg while the other leg stays straight with the foot flat or heel lifted; keep the chest tall and the loaded knee tracking over the toes.'),
  ('Bulgarian Bag Back Rack Reverse Lunge', 'Using a Bulgarian bag, held across the upper back, a staggered-stance lunge that steps backward into a split position -- generally easier to control than a forward lunge and gentler on the front knee.', 'Step straight back and lower under control until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the torso tall and the front knee tracking over the foot; drive through the front heel to return to standing.'),
  ('Bulgarian Bag Back Rack Russian Step Up', 'Using a Bulgarian bag, held across the upper back, an explosive step-up that drives the trailing leg up and forward off the box rather than just standing tall -- builds single-leg power.', 'Drive hard through the working leg on the box to bring the trailing knee up and slightly forward explosively; control the landing back down and reset before the next rep.'),
  ('Bulgarian Bag Back Rack Split Squat', 'Using a Bulgarian bag, held across the upper back, a stationary staggered-stance lower-body exercise -- builds single-leg strength and balance without the stepping motion of a lunge.', 'Take a split stance long enough that the front knee stays over the ankle at the bottom; lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; drive through the front foot to stand.'),
  ('Bulgarian Bag Back Rack Squat', 'Using a Bulgarian bag, held across the upper back, a foundational bend-the-knees-and-hips pattern that builds lower-body strength through the quads, glutes, and core.', 'Feet shoulder-width, toes slightly out; sit the hips back and down while keeping the chest tall; knees track over the toes; drive through the whole foot to stand.'),
  ('Bulgarian Bag Back Rack Step Up', 'Using a Bulgarian bag, held across the upper back, a single-leg strength staple that steps fully onto an elevated surface -- builds unilateral leg strength and stability.', 'Place the whole foot on the box; drive through that heel to stand fully upright without pushing off the trailing leg; control the descent back down.'),
  ('Bulgarian Bag Back Rack Walking Lunge', 'Using a Bulgarian bag, held across the upper back, a lunge performed continuously, stepping forward from one rep into the next -- adds a locomotion and balance demand to the standard lunge.', 'Step forward and lower until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; drive through the front leg to bring the back foot through into the next step, keeping the torso tall throughout.'),
  ('Bulgarian Bag Front Rack Squat', 'Using a Bulgarian bag, held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a foundational bend-the-knees-and-hips pattern that builds lower-body strength through the quads, glutes, and core.', 'Feet shoulder-width, toes slightly out; sit the hips back and down while keeping the chest tall; knees track over the toes; drive through the whole foot to stand.'),
  ('Bulgarian Bag Swing', 'Using a Bulgarian bag, a ballistic hip-hinge exercise that builds explosive hip power -- the hips do the work, not the arms or back.', 'Hike the weight back like a hinge, keeping the back flat; snap the hips forward explosively to swing it up to roughly chest height; let momentum bring it back down and absorb it into the next hinge.'),
  ('Bulgarian Bag Thruster', 'Using a Bulgarian bag, a full-body squat-to-press combination that builds power by linking the legs to an overhead press.', 'Hold the load at the shoulders; squat down keeping the torso upright; drive up explosively through the legs and press the load overhead in one fluid motion; return to the rack position under control.'),
  ('Cable Fire Hydrant', 'Using a cable machine, a hands-and-knees exercise that lifts the bent leg out to the side -- targets the outer hip/glute medius.', 'From hands and knees, keep the knee bent at roughly 90 degrees; lift the leg out to the side without rotating the hips or arching the back; lower under control.'),
  ('Cable Glute Kickback', 'Using a cable machine, a hip-extension isolation exercise that drives the leg back and up -- targets the glutes directly.', 'From hands and knees or standing at a cable, drive the working leg straight back and up squeezing the glute; avoid arching the low back to gain range; return under control.'),
  ('Cable Half Kneeling Pallof Press', 'Using a cable machine, from a half-kneeling position, an anti-rotation core exercise that presses a cable/band straight out from the chest while resisting the pull toward the anchor.', 'Stand side-on to the anchor with the band/cable at the chest; press straight out and hold, resisting the pull rotating the torso toward the anchor; return under control.'),
  ('Cable Hip Abduction', 'Using a cable machine, a hip isolation exercise that moves the leg away from the midline -- targets the outer hip and glute medius.', 'Keep the torso stable and avoid using momentum; move the working leg away from the body under control, feeling it in the outer hip; return under control.'),
  ('Cable Hip Adduction', 'Using a cable machine, a hip isolation exercise that draws the leg toward the midline -- targets the inner thigh (adductors).', 'Keep the torso stable and avoid using momentum; draw the working leg in toward the body under control, feeling it in the inner thigh; return under control.'),
  ('Cable Hip Rotation', 'Using a cable machine, a hip-mobility and control exercise that rotates the hip through an internal/external range against resistance.', 'Keep the pelvis square and stable; rotate the hip through the available range against the resistance under control; avoid compensating through the low back.'),
  ('Cable Kneeling Crunch', 'Using a cable machine, from a kneeling position, a cable/band crunch performed kneeling -- allows the abs to be loaded through a full range of trunk flexion.', 'Kneel below the anchor holding the rope/band at the head; crunch down by rounding the spine and bringing the elbows toward the knees; return under control without letting the hips shift back.'),
  ('Cable Pallof Press', 'Using a cable machine, an anti-rotation core exercise that presses a cable/band straight out from the chest while resisting the pull toward the anchor.', 'Stand side-on to the anchor with the band/cable at the chest; press straight out and hold, resisting the pull rotating the torso toward the anchor; return under control.'),
  ('Cable Prone Bench Hamstring Curl', 'Using a cable machine, a hamstring isolation exercise that curls the heels toward the glutes.', 'Keep the hips pressed down and square throughout; curl the heels toward the glutes squeezing the hamstrings at the top; lower under control without letting the hips pike up.'),
  ('Cable Prone Bench Single Leg Hamstring Curl', 'Using a cable machine, a single-leg hamstring curl that isolates one hamstring at a time.', 'Set up face down (or supported) with one leg engaged; curl the heel toward the glute, keeping the hips pressed down and square; lower under control.'),
  ('Cable Prone Hamstring Curl', 'Using a cable machine, a hamstring isolation exercise that curls the heels toward the glutes.', 'Keep the hips pressed down and square throughout; curl the heels toward the glutes squeezing the hamstrings at the top; lower under control without letting the hips pike up.'),
  ('Cable Prone Single Leg Hamstring Curl', 'Using a cable machine, a single-leg hamstring curl that isolates one hamstring at a time.', 'Set up face down (or supported) with one leg engaged; curl the heel toward the glute, keeping the hips pressed down and square; lower under control.'),
  ('Cable Pull Through', 'Using a cable machine, a cable/band hip-hinge exercise pulled from between the legs -- reinforces the hip-hinge pattern with continuous tension.', 'Facing away from the anchor, hinge the hips back with a flat back, letting the cable pull through between the legs; drive the hips forward to stand tall, squeezing the glutes at the top.'),
  ('Cable Quadruped Hip Abduction', 'Using a cable machine, a hands-and-knees exercise that lifts the knee out to the side -- targets the outer hip while the core resists rotation.', 'From hands and knees, keeping the knee bent, lift it out to the side without rotating the hips or shifting weight; lower under control.'),
  ('Cable Reverse Crunch', 'Using a cable machine, a crunch variation that curls the hips and knees up toward the chest instead of the shoulders up toward the knees -- emphasizes the lower abs.', 'Lying on the back, knees bent; curl the hips off the floor bringing the knees toward the chest, using the abs rather than momentum; lower under control.'),
  ('Cable Reverse Grip Lat Pulldown', 'Using a cable machine, gripped palms-up (reverse/underhand grip), a vertical pulling exercise that builds the lats and upper back through an overhead-to-chest range of motion.', 'Sit tall with the chest up; pull the bar/handle down toward the upper chest by driving the elbows down and back; control the return to a full overhead stretch.'),
  ('Cable Rope Pullover', 'Using a cable machine, a shoulder-extension exercise that moves a weight from overhead to the chest -- builds the lats and improves overhead shoulder mobility.', 'Lying on a bench (or floor), lower the weight back overhead with a slight elbow bend, feeling a stretch through the lats; pull it back over the chest by driving through the lats, not the arms.'),
  ('Cable Seated Crunch', 'Using a cable machine, a crunch performed in a seated position -- keeps continuous tension on the abs through the range of motion.', 'Sit tall, hands supported behind; curl the torso down and back, rounding through the spine; return by curling back up through the abs.'),
  ('Cable Single Leg Standing Bent Knee Hip External Rotation', 'Using a cable machine, a standing hip-mobility/strength exercise that rotates the bent leg outward against resistance -- targets the deep hip rotators.', 'Stand tall on the support leg with the working knee bent; rotate the knee outward against the resistance keeping the hips level; return under control.'),
  ('Cable Standing Single Leg Hamstring Curl', 'Using a cable machine, a standing, single-leg hamstring curl -- isolates the hamstring while also demanding standing-leg balance.', 'Stand tall on the support leg; curl the working heel toward the glute against the resistance, keeping the knee pointing down and the hips square; lower under control.'),
  ('Cable Straight Bar Pullover', 'Using a cable machine, a shoulder-extension exercise that moves a weight from overhead to the chest -- builds the lats and improves overhead shoulder mobility.', 'Lying on a bench (or floor), lower the weight back overhead with a slight elbow bend, feeling a stretch through the lats; pull it back over the chest by driving through the lats, not the arms.'),
  ('Cable Tall Kneeling Pallof Press', 'Using a cable machine, from a tall-kneeling position, an anti-rotation core exercise that presses a cable/band straight out from the chest while resisting the pull toward the anchor.', 'Stand side-on to the anchor with the band/cable at the chest; press straight out and hold, resisting the pull rotating the torso toward the anchor; return under control.'),
  ('Cable Tall Kneeling Pull Through', 'Using a cable machine, from a tall-kneeling position, a cable/band hip-hinge exercise pulled from between the legs -- reinforces the hip-hinge pattern with continuous tension.', 'Facing away from the anchor, hinge the hips back with a flat back, letting the cable pull through between the legs; drive the hips forward to stand tall, squeezing the glutes at the top.'),
  ('Cable V Grip Lat Pulldown', 'Using a cable machine, gripped on a close, neutral V-grip handle, a vertical pulling exercise that builds the lats and upper back through an overhead-to-chest range of motion.', 'Sit tall with the chest up; pull the bar/handle down toward the upper chest by driving the elbows down and back; control the return to a full overhead stretch.'),
  ('Cable V Grip Seated Floor Lat Pulldown', 'Using a cable machine, gripped on a close, neutral V-grip handle, a vertical pulling exercise that builds the lats and upper back through an overhead-to-chest range of motion.', 'Sit tall with the chest up; pull the bar/handle down toward the upper chest by driving the elbows down and back; control the return to a full overhead stretch.'),
  ('Cable V Grip Seated Low Row', 'Using a cable machine, gripped on a close, neutral V-grip handle, a band-resisted seated-style row pulled from a low angle -- targets the mid-back with continuous tension.', 'Sit or stand tall, arms extended toward the anchor; row the handles to the ribs by driving the elbows straight back and squeezing the shoulder blades together; return under control.'),
  ('Cable Wide Grip Lat Pulldown', 'Using a cable machine, gripped wide, a vertical pulling exercise that builds the lats and upper back through an overhead-to-chest range of motion.', 'Sit tall with the chest up; pull the bar/handle down toward the upper chest by driving the elbows down and back; control the return to a full overhead stretch.'),
  ('Clubbell Barbarian Alternating Forward Lunge', 'Using a clubbell, a staggered-stance lunge that steps forward into a split position -- builds single-leg strength and challenges balance through momentum.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step forward and lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the front knee tracking over the foot, not caving in; push back off the front foot to return to standing.'),
  ('Clubbell Barbarian Forward Lunge', 'Using a clubbell, a staggered-stance lunge that steps forward into a split position -- builds single-leg strength and challenges balance through momentum.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step forward and lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the front knee tracking over the foot, not caving in; push back off the front foot to return to standing.'),
  ('Clubbell Barbarian Squat', 'Using a clubbell, a squat performed holding the load balanced across the front of the shoulders (the clubbell/macebell "barbarian" hold), which challenges front-rack shoulder stability along with the legs.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Rest the implement across the front of both shoulders, elbows in front of the body; sit the hips back and down keeping the torso tall so the load stays balanced; drive up evenly through both feet.'),
  ('Clubbell Clean', 'Using a clubbell, a ballistic movement that brings the load from a hang or swing position up to the shoulder/rack in one fluid motion.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Hike the implement back to load the hips; snap the hips forward explosively and guide it up and around into the rack position at the shoulder; keep the transition smooth rather than muscling it up with the arm.'),
  ('Clubbell Order Alternating Cossack Squat', 'Using a clubbell, a wide-stance lateral squat that shifts weight fully onto one leg at a time -- builds hip and ankle mobility along with single-leg strength.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Take a wide stance, toes turned slightly out; sit the hips back and down over one leg while the other leg stays straight with the foot flat or heel lifted; keep the chest tall and the loaded knee tracking over the toes.'),
  ('Clubbell Order Alternating Curtsy Lunge', 'Using a clubbell, a rotational lunge that steps one leg diagonally behind the other -- targets the glutes (especially glute medius) through a diagonal plane.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step one leg diagonally behind and across the body, lowering the back knee toward the floor; keep the front knee tracking over the toes and the torso upright; push through the front leg to return to standing.'),
  ('Clubbell Order Alternating Forward Lunge', 'Using a clubbell, a staggered-stance lunge that steps forward into a split position -- builds single-leg strength and challenges balance through momentum.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step forward and lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the front knee tracking over the foot, not caving in; push back off the front foot to return to standing.'),
  ('Clubbell Order Alternating Lateral Lunge', 'Using a clubbell, a frontal-plane lunge that steps out to the side while the trailing leg stays straight -- builds hip and adductor strength outside the usual forward/back plane.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step out to the side and sit the hips back and down over the working leg; keep the trailing leg straight with the foot flat; keep the working knee tracking over the toes; push back through the working leg to return to center.'),
  ('Clubbell Order Alternating Reverse Lunge', 'Using a clubbell, a staggered-stance lunge that steps backward into a split position -- generally easier to control than a forward lunge and gentler on the front knee.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step straight back and lower under control until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the torso tall and the front knee tracking over the foot; drive through the front heel to return to standing.'),
  ('Clubbell Order Alternating Russian Step Up', 'Using a clubbell, an explosive step-up that drives the trailing leg up and forward off the box rather than just standing tall -- builds single-leg power.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Drive hard through the working leg on the box to bring the trailing knee up and slightly forward explosively; control the landing back down and reset before the next rep.'),
  ('Clubbell Order Alternating Step Up', 'Using a clubbell, a single-leg strength staple that steps fully onto an elevated surface -- builds unilateral leg strength and stability.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Place the whole foot on the box; drive through that heel to stand fully upright without pushing off the trailing leg; control the descent back down.'),
  ('Clubbell Order Cossack Squat', 'Using a clubbell, a wide-stance lateral squat that shifts weight fully onto one leg at a time -- builds hip and ankle mobility along with single-leg strength.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Take a wide stance, toes turned slightly out; sit the hips back and down over one leg while the other leg stays straight with the foot flat or heel lifted; keep the chest tall and the loaded knee tracking over the toes.'),
  ('Clubbell Order Curtsy Lunge', 'Using a clubbell, a rotational lunge that steps one leg diagonally behind the other -- targets the glutes (especially glute medius) through a diagonal plane.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step one leg diagonally behind and across the body, lowering the back knee toward the floor; keep the front knee tracking over the toes and the torso upright; push through the front leg to return to standing.'),
  ('Clubbell Order Cyclist Squat', 'Using a clubbell, a narrow-stance, heels-elevated squat that emphasizes the quads by allowing a very upright torso and deep knee bend.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Heels elevated (plate, wedge, or shoes), feet close together; sit straight down keeping the torso upright; let the knees travel forward over the toes; drive through the front of the foot to stand.'),
  ('Clubbell Order Foot Elevated Knee Over Toe Split Squat', 'Using a clubbell, with the rear/working foot elevated to increase the range and demand, a split squat variation that deliberately lets the front knee travel forward past the toes -- builds knee and ankle resilience through a deeper range.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Take a shorter stance than a normal split squat; let the front knee travel forward over and past the toes as you lower, keeping the heel down; control the descent and drive back up through the front leg.'),
  ('Clubbell Order Forward Lunge', 'Using a clubbell, a staggered-stance lunge that steps forward into a split position -- builds single-leg strength and challenges balance through momentum.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step forward and lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the front knee tracking over the foot, not caving in; push back off the front foot to return to standing.'),
  ('Clubbell Order Knee Over Toe Split Squat', 'Using a clubbell, a split squat variation that deliberately lets the front knee travel forward past the toes -- builds knee and ankle resilience through a deeper range.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Take a shorter stance than a normal split squat; let the front knee travel forward over and past the toes as you lower, keeping the heel down; control the descent and drive back up through the front leg.'),
  ('Clubbell Order Lateral Lunge', 'Using a clubbell, a frontal-plane lunge that steps out to the side while the trailing leg stays straight -- builds hip and adductor strength outside the usual forward/back plane.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step out to the side and sit the hips back and down over the working leg; keep the trailing leg straight with the foot flat; keep the working knee tracking over the toes; push back through the working leg to return to center.'),
  ('Clubbell Order Reverse Lunge', 'Using a clubbell, a staggered-stance lunge that steps backward into a split position -- generally easier to control than a forward lunge and gentler on the front knee.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step straight back and lower under control until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the torso tall and the front knee tracking over the foot; drive through the front heel to return to standing.'),
  ('Clubbell Order Russian Step Up', 'Using a clubbell, an explosive step-up that drives the trailing leg up and forward off the box rather than just standing tall -- builds single-leg power.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Drive hard through the working leg on the box to bring the trailing knee up and slightly forward explosively; control the landing back down and reset before the next rep.'),
  ('Clubbell Order Split Squat', 'Using a clubbell, a stationary staggered-stance lower-body exercise -- builds single-leg strength and balance without the stepping motion of a lunge.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Take a split stance long enough that the front knee stays over the ankle at the bottom; lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; drive through the front foot to stand.'),
  ('Clubbell Order Squat', 'Using a clubbell, a squat performed holding the clubbell/macebell in the "order" position (upright at the shoulder) -- adds an asymmetric core-stability demand on top of the squat.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Keep the implement stacked vertically at the shoulder without letting it drift forward or out to the side; sit the hips back and down keeping the torso tall; brace the core to resist the pull of the offset load.'),
  ('Clubbell Order Step Up', 'Using a clubbell, a single-leg strength staple that steps fully onto an elevated surface -- builds unilateral leg strength and stability.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Place the whole foot on the box; drive through that heel to stand fully upright without pushing off the trailing leg; control the descent back down.'),
  ('Clubbell Order Walking Lunge', 'Using a clubbell, a lunge performed continuously, stepping forward from one rep into the next -- adds a locomotion and balance demand to the standard lunge.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step forward and lower until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; drive through the front leg to bring the back foot through into the next step, keeping the torso tall throughout.'),
  ('Clubbell Pullover', 'Using a clubbell, a shoulder-extension exercise that moves a weight from overhead to the chest -- builds the lats and improves overhead shoulder mobility.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Lying on a bench (or floor), lower the weight back overhead with a slight elbow bend, feeling a stretch through the lats; pull it back over the chest by driving through the lats, not the arms.'),
  ('Clubbell Shoulder Park Squat', 'Using a clubbell, a squat holding the clubbell "parked" horizontally across one shoulder -- an asymmetric-load core and stability challenge layered on the squat.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Rest the implement across one shoulder, gripping the handle to keep it stable; brace the core to resist leaning toward the load; sit the hips back and down keeping the torso as upright as the offset load allows.'),
  ('Clubbell Swing', 'Using a clubbell, a ballistic hip-hinge exercise that builds explosive hip power -- the hips do the work, not the arms or back.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Hike the weight back like a hinge, keeping the back flat; snap the hips forward explosively to swing it up to roughly chest height; let momentum bring it back down and absorb it into the next hinge.'),
