  ('Sandbag Front Rack Curtsy Lunge', 'Using a sandbag, held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a rotational lunge that steps one leg diagonally behind the other -- targets the glutes (especially glute medius) through a diagonal plane.', 'Expect the sandbag''s shifting weight to add an extra stability demand compared to a rigid implement. Step one leg diagonally behind and across the body, lowering the back knee toward the floor; keep the front knee tracking over the toes and the torso upright; push through the front leg to return to standing.'),
  ('Sandbag Front Rack Cyclist Squat', 'Using a sandbag, held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a narrow-stance, heels-elevated squat that emphasizes the quads by allowing a very upright torso and deep knee bend.', 'Expect the sandbag''s shifting weight to add an extra stability demand compared to a rigid implement. Heels elevated (plate, wedge, or shoes), feet close together; sit straight down keeping the torso upright; let the knees travel forward over the toes; drive through the front of the foot to stand.'),
  ('Sandbag Front Rack Foot Elevated Cossack Squat', 'Using a sandbag, held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a wide-stance lateral squat that shifts weight fully onto one leg at a time -- builds hip and ankle mobility along with single-leg strength.', 'Expect the sandbag''s shifting weight to add an extra stability demand compared to a rigid implement. Take a wide stance, toes turned slightly out; sit the hips back and down over one leg while the other leg stays straight with the foot flat or heel lifted; keep the chest tall and the loaded knee tracking over the toes.'),
  ('Sandbag Front Rack Foot Elevated Knee Over Toe Split Squat', 'Using a sandbag, held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a split squat variation that deliberately lets the front knee travel forward past the toes -- builds knee and ankle resilience through a deeper range.', 'Expect the sandbag''s shifting weight to add an extra stability demand compared to a rigid implement. Take a shorter stance than a normal split squat; let the front knee travel forward over and past the toes as you lower, keeping the heel down; control the descent and drive back up through the front leg.'),
