  ('Sandbag Front Rack Walking Lunge', 'Using a sandbag, held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a lunge performed continuously, stepping forward from one rep into the next -- adds a locomotion and balance demand to the standard lunge.', 'Expect the sandbag''s shifting weight to add an extra stability demand compared to a rigid implement. Step forward and lower until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; drive through the front leg to bring the back foot through into the next step, keeping the torso tall throughout.'),
  ('Sandbag Thruster', 'Using a sandbag, a full-body squat-to-press combination that builds power by linking the legs to an overhead press.', 'Expect the sandbag''s shifting weight to add an extra stability demand compared to a rigid implement. Hold the load at the shoulders; squat down keeping the torso upright; drive up explosively through the legs and press the load overhead in one fluid motion; return to the rack position under control.'),
  ('Single Arm Barbell Suitcase Deadlift', 'Using a barbell, held down at your side like a suitcase, a deadlift performed with the load at one or both sides of the body (like picking up a suitcase) -- adds an anti-lateral-flexion core demand. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Stand the load beside the feet; hinge the hips back with a flat back to grip it; drive through the floor to stand tall while resisting leaning toward the load; lower back under control.'),
  ('Single Arm Cable Half Kneeling Lat Pulldown', 'Using a cable machine, from a half-kneeling position, a vertical pulling exercise that builds the lats and upper back through an overhead-to-chest range of motion. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Sit tall with the chest up; pull the bar/handle down toward the upper chest by driving the elbows down and back; control the return to a full overhead stretch.'),
  ('Single Arm Cable Half Kneeling Low Row', 'Using a cable machine, from a half-kneeling position, a band-resisted seated-style row pulled from a low angle -- targets the mid-back with continuous tension. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Sit or stand tall, arms extended toward the anchor; row the handles to the ribs by driving the elbows straight back and squeezing the shoulder blades together; return under control.'),
  ('Single Arm Cable Seated Floor Lat Pulldown', 'Using a cable machine, a vertical pulling exercise that builds the lats and upper back through an overhead-to-chest range of motion. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Sit tall with the chest up; pull the bar/handle down toward the upper chest by driving the elbows down and back; control the return to a full overhead stretch.'),
  ('Single Arm Cable Shotgun Row', 'Using a cable machine, a single-arm cable row performed in a split or staggered stance -- builds back strength with an added stability demand. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Split stance, hinge slightly forward with a flat back; row the handle to the ribs, driving the elbow straight back; control the return to a full stretch.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Barbarian Squat', 'Using a clubbell, a squat performed holding the load balanced across the front of the shoulders (the clubbell/macebell "barbarian" hold), which challenges front-rack shoulder stability along with the legs. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Rest the implement across the front of both shoulders, elbows in front of the body; sit the hips back and down keeping the torso tall so the load stays balanced; drive up evenly through both feet.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Clean', 'Using a clubbell, a ballistic movement that brings the load from a hang or swing position up to the shoulder/rack in one fluid motion. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Hike the implement back to load the hips; snap the hips forward explosively and guide it up and around into the rack position at the shoulder; keep the transition smooth rather than muscling it up with the arm.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Inside Pullover', 'Using a clubbell, a shoulder-extension exercise that moves a weight from overhead to the chest -- builds the lats and improves overhead shoulder mobility. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Lying on a bench (or floor), lower the weight back overhead with a slight elbow bend, feeling a stretch through the lats; pull it back over the chest by driving through the lats, not the arms.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Order Alternating Cossack Squat', 'Using a clubbell, a wide-stance lateral squat that shifts weight fully onto one leg at a time -- builds hip and ankle mobility along with single-leg strength. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Take a wide stance, toes turned slightly out; sit the hips back and down over one leg while the other leg stays straight with the foot flat or heel lifted; keep the chest tall and the loaded knee tracking over the toes.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Order Alternating Curtsy Lunge', 'Using a clubbell, a rotational lunge that steps one leg diagonally behind the other -- targets the glutes (especially glute medius) through a diagonal plane. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step one leg diagonally behind and across the body, lowering the back knee toward the floor; keep the front knee tracking over the toes and the torso upright; push through the front leg to return to standing.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Order Alternating Forward Lunge', 'Using a clubbell, a staggered-stance lunge that steps forward into a split position -- builds single-leg strength and challenges balance through momentum. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step forward and lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the front knee tracking over the foot, not caving in; push back off the front foot to return to standing.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Order Alternating Lateral Lunge', 'Using a clubbell, a frontal-plane lunge that steps out to the side while the trailing leg stays straight -- builds hip and adductor strength outside the usual forward/back plane. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step out to the side and sit the hips back and down over the working leg; keep the trailing leg straight with the foot flat; keep the working knee tracking over the toes; push back through the working leg to return to center.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Order Alternating Reverse Lunge', 'Using a clubbell, a staggered-stance lunge that steps backward into a split position -- generally easier to control than a forward lunge and gentler on the front knee. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step straight back and lower under control until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the torso tall and the front knee tracking over the foot; drive through the front heel to return to standing.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Order Contralateral Cossack Squat', 'Using a clubbell, a wide-stance lateral squat that shifts weight fully onto one leg at a time -- builds hip and ankle mobility along with single-leg strength. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Take a wide stance, toes turned slightly out; sit the hips back and down over one leg while the other leg stays straight with the foot flat or heel lifted; keep the chest tall and the loaded knee tracking over the toes.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Order Contralateral Curtsy Lunge', 'Using a clubbell, a rotational lunge that steps one leg diagonally behind the other -- targets the glutes (especially glute medius) through a diagonal plane. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step one leg diagonally behind and across the body, lowering the back knee toward the floor; keep the front knee tracking over the toes and the torso upright; push through the front leg to return to standing.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Order Contralateral Forward Lunge', 'Using a clubbell, a staggered-stance lunge that steps forward into a split position -- builds single-leg strength and challenges balance through momentum. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step forward and lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the front knee tracking over the foot, not caving in; push back off the front foot to return to standing.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Order Contralateral Knee Over Toe Split Squat', 'Using a clubbell, a split squat variation that deliberately lets the front knee travel forward past the toes -- builds knee and ankle resilience through a deeper range. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Take a shorter stance than a normal split squat; let the front knee travel forward over and past the toes as you lower, keeping the heel down; control the descent and drive back up through the front leg.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Order Contralateral Lateral Lunge', 'Using a clubbell, a frontal-plane lunge that steps out to the side while the trailing leg stays straight -- builds hip and adductor strength outside the usual forward/back plane. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step out to the side and sit the hips back and down over the working leg; keep the trailing leg straight with the foot flat; keep the working knee tracking over the toes; push back through the working leg to return to center.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Order Contralateral Reverse Lunge', 'Using a clubbell, a staggered-stance lunge that steps backward into a split position -- generally easier to control than a forward lunge and gentler on the front knee. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step straight back and lower under control until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the torso tall and the front knee tracking over the foot; drive through the front heel to return to standing.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Order Contralateral Russian Step Up', 'Using a clubbell, an explosive step-up that drives the trailing leg up and forward off the box rather than just standing tall -- builds single-leg power. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Drive hard through the working leg on the box to bring the trailing knee up and slightly forward explosively; control the landing back down and reset before the next rep.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Order Contralateral Split Squat', 'Using a clubbell, a stationary staggered-stance lower-body exercise -- builds single-leg strength and balance without the stepping motion of a lunge. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Take a split stance long enough that the front knee stays over the ankle at the bottom; lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; drive through the front foot to stand.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Order Contralateral Step Up', 'Using a clubbell, a single-leg strength staple that steps fully onto an elevated surface -- builds unilateral leg strength and stability. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Place the whole foot on the box; drive through that heel to stand fully upright without pushing off the trailing leg; control the descent back down.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Order Foot Elevated Contralateral Knee Over Toe Split Squat', 'Using a clubbell, with the rear/working foot elevated to increase the range and demand, a split squat variation that deliberately lets the front knee travel forward past the toes -- builds knee and ankle resilience through a deeper range. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Take a shorter stance than a normal split squat; let the front knee travel forward over and past the toes as you lower, keeping the heel down; control the descent and drive back up through the front leg.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Order Foot Elevated Ipsilateral Knee Over Toe Split Squat', 'Using a clubbell, with the rear/working foot elevated to increase the range and demand, a split squat variation that deliberately lets the front knee travel forward past the toes -- builds knee and ankle resilience through a deeper range. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Take a shorter stance than a normal split squat; let the front knee travel forward over and past the toes as you lower, keeping the heel down; control the descent and drive back up through the front leg.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Order Ipsilateral Cossack Squat', 'Using a clubbell, a wide-stance lateral squat that shifts weight fully onto one leg at a time -- builds hip and ankle mobility along with single-leg strength. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Take a wide stance, toes turned slightly out; sit the hips back and down over one leg while the other leg stays straight with the foot flat or heel lifted; keep the chest tall and the loaded knee tracking over the toes.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Order Ipsilateral Curtsy Lunge', 'Using a clubbell, a rotational lunge that steps one leg diagonally behind the other -- targets the glutes (especially glute medius) through a diagonal plane. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step one leg diagonally behind and across the body, lowering the back knee toward the floor; keep the front knee tracking over the toes and the torso upright; push through the front leg to return to standing.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Order Ipsilateral Forward Lunge', 'Using a clubbell, a staggered-stance lunge that steps forward into a split position -- builds single-leg strength and challenges balance through momentum. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step forward and lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the front knee tracking over the foot, not caving in; push back off the front foot to return to standing.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Order Ipsilateral Knee Over Toe Split Squat', 'Using a clubbell, a split squat variation that deliberately lets the front knee travel forward past the toes -- builds knee and ankle resilience through a deeper range. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Take a shorter stance than a normal split squat; let the front knee travel forward over and past the toes as you lower, keeping the heel down; control the descent and drive back up through the front leg.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Order Ipsilateral Lateral Lunge', 'Using a clubbell, a frontal-plane lunge that steps out to the side while the trailing leg stays straight -- builds hip and adductor strength outside the usual forward/back plane. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step out to the side and sit the hips back and down over the working leg; keep the trailing leg straight with the foot flat; keep the working knee tracking over the toes; push back through the working leg to return to center.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Order Ipsilateral Reverse Lunge', 'Using a clubbell, a staggered-stance lunge that steps backward into a split position -- generally easier to control than a forward lunge and gentler on the front knee. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step straight back and lower under control until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the torso tall and the front knee tracking over the foot; drive through the front heel to return to standing.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Order Ipsilateral Russian Step Up', 'Using a clubbell, an explosive step-up that drives the trailing leg up and forward off the box rather than just standing tall -- builds single-leg power. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Drive hard through the working leg on the box to bring the trailing knee up and slightly forward explosively; control the landing back down and reset before the next rep.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Order Ipsilateral Split Squat', 'Using a clubbell, a stationary staggered-stance lower-body exercise -- builds single-leg strength and balance without the stepping motion of a lunge. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Take a split stance long enough that the front knee stays over the ankle at the bottom; lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; drive through the front foot to stand.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Order Ipsilateral Step Up', 'Using a clubbell, a single-leg strength staple that steps fully onto an elevated surface -- builds unilateral leg strength and stability. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Place the whole foot on the box; drive through that heel to stand fully upright without pushing off the trailing leg; control the descent back down.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Order Squat', 'Using a clubbell, a squat performed holding the clubbell/macebell in the "order" position (upright at the shoulder) -- adds an asymmetric core-stability demand on top of the squat. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Keep the implement stacked vertically at the shoulder without letting it drift forward or out to the side; sit the hips back and down keeping the torso tall; brace the core to resist the pull of the offset load.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Order Walking Lunge', 'Using a clubbell, a lunge performed continuously, stepping forward from one rep into the next -- adds a locomotion and balance demand to the standard lunge. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step forward and lower until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; drive through the front leg to bring the back foot through into the next step, keeping the torso tall throughout.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Outside Pullover', 'Using a clubbell, a shoulder-extension exercise that moves a weight from overhead to the chest -- builds the lats and improves overhead shoulder mobility. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Lying on a bench (or floor), lower the weight back overhead with a slight elbow bend, feeling a stretch through the lats; pull it back over the chest by driving through the lats, not the arms.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Pullover', 'Using a clubbell, a shoulder-extension exercise that moves a weight from overhead to the chest -- builds the lats and improves overhead shoulder mobility. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Lying on a bench (or floor), lower the weight back overhead with a slight elbow bend, feeling a stretch through the lats; pull it back over the chest by driving through the lats, not the arms.'),
  ('Single Arm Clubbell Shoulder Park Squat', 'Using a clubbell, a squat holding the clubbell "parked" horizontally across one shoulder -- an asymmetric-load core and stability challenge layered on the squat. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Let the clubbell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Rest the implement across one shoulder, gripping the handle to keep it stable; brace the core to resist leaning toward the load; sit the hips back and down keeping the torso as upright as the offset load allows.'),
  ('Single Arm Dumbbell Bent Over Row', 'Using a dumbbell (or a pair), a classic hip-hinged row that builds the whole back -- lats, upper back, and rear shoulders. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Hinge at the hips with a flat back roughly parallel to the floor, core braced; row the weight to the lower ribs by driving the elbows back; lower under control without the torso swinging.'),
  ('Single Arm Dumbbell Cyclist Thruster', 'Using a dumbbell (or a pair), a heels-elevated squat-to-press combination that emphasizes the quads through the squat and adds an overhead pressing finish. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Heels elevated, feet close together; squat down keeping the torso upright, then drive up explosively and press the load overhead in one fluid motion; lower back to the rack position under control.'),
  ('Single Arm Dumbbell Front Rack Alternating Cossack Squat', 'Using a dumbbell (or a pair), held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a wide-stance lateral squat that shifts weight fully onto one leg at a time -- builds hip and ankle mobility along with single-leg strength. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Take a wide stance, toes turned slightly out; sit the hips back and down over one leg while the other leg stays straight with the foot flat or heel lifted; keep the chest tall and the loaded knee tracking over the toes.'),
