  ('Single Arm Dumbbell Front Rack Contralateral Step Up', 'Using a dumbbell (or a pair), held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a single-leg strength staple that steps fully onto an elevated surface -- builds unilateral leg strength and stability. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Place the whole foot on the box; drive through that heel to stand fully upright without pushing off the trailing leg; control the descent back down.'),
  ('Single Arm Dumbbell Front Rack Cyclist Squat', 'Using a dumbbell (or a pair), held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a narrow-stance, heels-elevated squat that emphasizes the quads by allowing a very upright torso and deep knee bend. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Heels elevated (plate, wedge, or shoes), feet close together; sit straight down keeping the torso upright; let the knees travel forward over the toes; drive through the front of the foot to stand.'),
  ('Single Arm Dumbbell Front Rack Foot Elevated Contralateral Knee Over Toe Split Squat', 'Using a dumbbell (or a pair), held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a split squat variation that deliberately lets the front knee travel forward past the toes -- builds knee and ankle resilience through a deeper range. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Take a shorter stance than a normal split squat; let the front knee travel forward over and past the toes as you lower, keeping the heel down; control the descent and drive back up through the front leg.'),
  ('Single Arm Dumbbell Front Rack Foot Elevated Ipsilateral Knee Over Toe Split Squat', 'Using a dumbbell (or a pair), held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a split squat variation that deliberately lets the front knee travel forward past the toes -- builds knee and ankle resilience through a deeper range. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Take a shorter stance than a normal split squat; let the front knee travel forward over and past the toes as you lower, keeping the heel down; control the descent and drive back up through the front leg.'),
  ('Single Arm Dumbbell Front Rack Ipsilateral Cossack Squat', 'Using a dumbbell (or a pair), held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a wide-stance lateral squat that shifts weight fully onto one leg at a time -- builds hip and ankle mobility along with single-leg strength. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Take a wide stance, toes turned slightly out; sit the hips back and down over one leg while the other leg stays straight with the foot flat or heel lifted; keep the chest tall and the loaded knee tracking over the toes.'),
  ('Single Arm Dumbbell Front Rack Ipsilateral Curtsy Lunge', 'Using a dumbbell (or a pair), held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a rotational lunge that steps one leg diagonally behind the other -- targets the glutes (especially glute medius) through a diagonal plane. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Step one leg diagonally behind and across the body, lowering the back knee toward the floor; keep the front knee tracking over the toes and the torso upright; push through the front leg to return to standing.'),
  ('Single Arm Dumbbell Front Rack Ipsilateral Forward Lunge', 'Using a dumbbell (or a pair), held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a staggered-stance lunge that steps forward into a split position -- builds single-leg strength and challenges balance through momentum. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Step forward and lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the front knee tracking over the foot, not caving in; push back off the front foot to return to standing.'),
  ('Single Arm Dumbbell Front Rack Ipsilateral Knee Over Toe Split Squat', 'Using a dumbbell (or a pair), held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a split squat variation that deliberately lets the front knee travel forward past the toes -- builds knee and ankle resilience through a deeper range. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Take a shorter stance than a normal split squat; let the front knee travel forward over and past the toes as you lower, keeping the heel down; control the descent and drive back up through the front leg.'),
  ('Single Arm Dumbbell Front Rack Ipsilateral Lateral Lunge', 'Using a dumbbell (or a pair), held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a frontal-plane lunge that steps out to the side while the trailing leg stays straight -- builds hip and adductor strength outside the usual forward/back plane. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Step out to the side and sit the hips back and down over the working leg; keep the trailing leg straight with the foot flat; keep the working knee tracking over the toes; push back through the working leg to return to center.'),
  ('Single Arm Dumbbell Front Rack Ipsilateral Reverse Lunge', 'Using a dumbbell (or a pair), held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a staggered-stance lunge that steps backward into a split position -- generally easier to control than a forward lunge and gentler on the front knee. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Step straight back and lower under control until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the torso tall and the front knee tracking over the foot; drive through the front heel to return to standing.'),
  ('Single Arm Dumbbell Front Rack Ipsilateral Russian Step Up', 'Using a dumbbell (or a pair), held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, an explosive step-up that drives the trailing leg up and forward off the box rather than just standing tall -- builds single-leg power. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Drive hard through the working leg on the box to bring the trailing knee up and slightly forward explosively; control the landing back down and reset before the next rep.'),
  ('Single Arm Dumbbell Front Rack Ipsilateral Split Squat', 'Using a dumbbell (or a pair), held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a stationary staggered-stance lower-body exercise -- builds single-leg strength and balance without the stepping motion of a lunge. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Take a split stance long enough that the front knee stays over the ankle at the bottom; lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; drive through the front foot to stand.'),
  ('Single Arm Dumbbell Front Rack Ipsilateral Step Up', 'Using a dumbbell (or a pair), held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a single-leg strength staple that steps fully onto an elevated surface -- builds unilateral leg strength and stability. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Place the whole foot on the box; drive through that heel to stand fully upright without pushing off the trailing leg; control the descent back down.'),
  ('Single Arm Dumbbell Front Rack Squat', 'Using a dumbbell (or a pair), held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a foundational bend-the-knees-and-hips pattern that builds lower-body strength through the quads, glutes, and core. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Feet shoulder-width, toes slightly out; sit the hips back and down while keeping the chest tall; knees track over the toes; drive through the whole foot to stand.'),
  ('Single Arm Dumbbell Front Rack Walking Lunge', 'Using a dumbbell (or a pair), held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a lunge performed continuously, stepping forward from one rep into the next -- adds a locomotion and balance demand to the standard lunge. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Step forward and lower until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; drive through the front leg to bring the back foot through into the next step, keeping the torso tall throughout.'),
  ('Single Arm Dumbbell Incline Bench Prone Row', 'Using a dumbbell (or a pair), set up on an incline bench, a row performed lying face-down on an incline bench -- removes the lower back from the equation to isolate the upper back and lats. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Chest supported on the bench, let the arms hang; row by driving the elbows back and squeezing the shoulder blades together; lower under control through a full stretch.'),
  ('Single Arm Dumbbell Knee Supported Row', 'Using a dumbbell (or a pair), a single-arm row supported by a bench, which stabilizes the torso and isolates one side of the back at a time. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Support the same-side hand and knee on the bench, flat back; row the weight to the ribs by driving the elbow back; lower under control through a full stretch.'),
  ('Single Arm Dumbbell Pendlay Row', 'Using a dumbbell (or a pair), a bent over row performed from a dead stop on the floor each rep -- removes momentum and emphasizes strict pulling strength. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Hinge over with a flat back roughly parallel to the floor; pull explosively from a dead stop into the lower ribs, driving the elbows back; return the weight fully to the floor each rep.'),
  ('Single Arm Dumbbell Suitcase Alternating Curtsy Lunge', 'Using a dumbbell (or a pair), held down at your side like a suitcase, a rotational lunge that steps one leg diagonally behind the other -- targets the glutes (especially glute medius) through a diagonal plane. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Step one leg diagonally behind and across the body, lowering the back knee toward the floor; keep the front knee tracking over the toes and the torso upright; push through the front leg to return to standing.'),
  ('Single Arm Dumbbell Suitcase Alternating Forward Lunge', 'Using a dumbbell (or a pair), held down at your side like a suitcase, a staggered-stance lunge that steps forward into a split position -- builds single-leg strength and challenges balance through momentum. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Step forward and lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the front knee tracking over the foot, not caving in; push back off the front foot to return to standing.'),
  ('Single Arm Dumbbell Suitcase Alternating Reverse Lunge', 'Using a dumbbell (or a pair), held down at your side like a suitcase, a staggered-stance lunge that steps backward into a split position -- generally easier to control than a forward lunge and gentler on the front knee. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Step straight back and lower under control until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the torso tall and the front knee tracking over the foot; drive through the front heel to return to standing.'),
  ('Single Arm Dumbbell Suitcase Contralateral Curtsy Lunge', 'Using a dumbbell (or a pair), held down at your side like a suitcase, a rotational lunge that steps one leg diagonally behind the other -- targets the glutes (especially glute medius) through a diagonal plane. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Step one leg diagonally behind and across the body, lowering the back knee toward the floor; keep the front knee tracking over the toes and the torso upright; push through the front leg to return to standing.'),
  ('Single Arm Dumbbell Suitcase Contralateral Forward Lunge', 'Using a dumbbell (or a pair), held down at your side like a suitcase, a staggered-stance lunge that steps forward into a split position -- builds single-leg strength and challenges balance through momentum. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Step forward and lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the front knee tracking over the foot, not caving in; push back off the front foot to return to standing.'),
