  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Front Rack Ipsilateral Cossack Squat', 'Using a kettlebell, held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a wide-stance lateral squat that shifts weight fully onto one leg at a time -- builds hip and ankle mobility along with single-leg strength. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Take a wide stance, toes turned slightly out; sit the hips back and down over one leg while the other leg stays straight with the foot flat or heel lifted; keep the chest tall and the loaded knee tracking over the toes.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Front Rack Ipsilateral Curtsy Lunge', 'Using a kettlebell, held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a rotational lunge that steps one leg diagonally behind the other -- targets the glutes (especially glute medius) through a diagonal plane. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Step one leg diagonally behind and across the body, lowering the back knee toward the floor; keep the front knee tracking over the toes and the torso upright; push through the front leg to return to standing.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Front Rack Ipsilateral Forward Lunge', 'Using a kettlebell, held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a staggered-stance lunge that steps forward into a split position -- builds single-leg strength and challenges balance through momentum. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Step forward and lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the front knee tracking over the foot, not caving in; push back off the front foot to return to standing.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Front Rack Ipsilateral Knee Over Toe Split Squat', 'Using a kettlebell, held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a split squat variation that deliberately lets the front knee travel forward past the toes -- builds knee and ankle resilience through a deeper range. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Take a shorter stance than a normal split squat; let the front knee travel forward over and past the toes as you lower, keeping the heel down; control the descent and drive back up through the front leg.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Front Rack Ipsilateral Lateral Lunge', 'Using a kettlebell, held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a frontal-plane lunge that steps out to the side while the trailing leg stays straight -- builds hip and adductor strength outside the usual forward/back plane. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Step out to the side and sit the hips back and down over the working leg; keep the trailing leg straight with the foot flat; keep the working knee tracking over the toes; push back through the working leg to return to center.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Front Rack Ipsilateral Reverse Lunge', 'Using a kettlebell, held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a staggered-stance lunge that steps backward into a split position -- generally easier to control than a forward lunge and gentler on the front knee. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Step straight back and lower under control until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the torso tall and the front knee tracking over the foot; drive through the front heel to return to standing.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Front Rack Ipsilateral Russian Step Up', 'Using a kettlebell, held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, an explosive step-up that drives the trailing leg up and forward off the box rather than just standing tall -- builds single-leg power. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Drive hard through the working leg on the box to bring the trailing knee up and slightly forward explosively; control the landing back down and reset before the next rep.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Front Rack Ipsilateral Split Squat', 'Using a kettlebell, held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a stationary staggered-stance lower-body exercise -- builds single-leg strength and balance without the stepping motion of a lunge. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Take a split stance long enough that the front knee stays over the ankle at the bottom; lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; drive through the front foot to stand.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Front Rack Ipsilateral Step Up', 'Using a kettlebell, held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a single-leg strength staple that steps fully onto an elevated surface -- builds unilateral leg strength and stability. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Place the whole foot on the box; drive through that heel to stand fully upright without pushing off the trailing leg; control the descent back down.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Front Rack Squat', 'Using a kettlebell, held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a foundational bend-the-knees-and-hips pattern that builds lower-body strength through the quads, glutes, and core. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Feet shoulder-width, toes slightly out; sit the hips back and down while keeping the chest tall; knees track over the toes; drive through the whole foot to stand.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Front Rack Walking Lunge', 'Using a kettlebell, held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a lunge performed continuously, stepping forward from one rep into the next -- adds a locomotion and balance demand to the standard lunge. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Step forward and lower until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; drive through the front leg to bring the back foot through into the next step, keeping the torso tall throughout.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Incline Bench Prone Row', 'Using a kettlebell, set up on an incline bench, a row performed lying face-down on an incline bench -- removes the lower back from the equation to isolate the upper back and lats. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Chest supported on the bench, let the arms hang; row by driving the elbows back and squeezing the shoulder blades together; lower under control through a full stretch.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Knee Supported Row', 'Using a kettlebell, a single-arm row supported by a bench, which stabilizes the torso and isolates one side of the back at a time. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Support the same-side hand and knee on the bench, flat back; row the weight to the ribs by driving the elbow back; lower under control through a full stretch.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Otis Up', 'Using a kettlebell, a get-up-style flow move that presses the weight overhead from lying and rolls up to standing (or a chosen partial range) while keeping the arm locked out -- builds shoulder stability and total-body coordination. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Press the weight overhead first and keep the arm locked out through the whole movement; roll and stand up (or move through the chosen segment) without letting the weight drift from directly over the shoulder; reverse under control.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Outside Swing', 'Using a kettlebell, a single-arm kettlebell swing performed alongside the body rather than between the legs -- adds an anti-rotation demand to the swing. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Hike the bell back beside the hip; snap the hips forward to swing it up to chest height, keeping the shoulders square; control the bell back down alongside the body.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Pendlay Row', 'Using a kettlebell, a bent over row performed from a dead stop on the floor each rep -- removes momentum and emphasizes strict pulling strength. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Hinge over with a flat back roughly parallel to the floor; pull explosively from a dead stop into the lower ribs, driving the elbows back; return the weight fully to the floor each rep.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Prone Row', 'Using a kettlebell, a row performed face-down on a bench -- takes the lower back out of the movement to isolate the upper back. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Chest supported, let the arms hang fully; row by driving the elbows back and squeezing the shoulder blades together; lower under control through a full stretch.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Rotational Thruster', 'Using a kettlebell, a thruster combined with a rotation, adding a core and hip-power demand to the squat-to-press pattern. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Squat down keeping the torso upright; drive up explosively, rotating through the hips and pressing the load overhead in one fluid motion; return to the rack position under control.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Start Stop Swing', 'Using a kettlebell, a kettlebell swing performed with a dead-stop pause between reps -- removes momentum and reinforces the hip-hinge pattern. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Hike the bell back between the legs like a hinge; snap the hips forward to swing it to chest height; let it settle to a dead stop on the ground before the next rep.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Suitcase Alternating Curtsy Lunge', 'Using a kettlebell, held down at your side like a suitcase, a rotational lunge that steps one leg diagonally behind the other -- targets the glutes (especially glute medius) through a diagonal plane. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Step one leg diagonally behind and across the body, lowering the back knee toward the floor; keep the front knee tracking over the toes and the torso upright; push through the front leg to return to standing.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Suitcase Alternating Forward Lunge', 'Using a kettlebell, held down at your side like a suitcase, a staggered-stance lunge that steps forward into a split position -- builds single-leg strength and challenges balance through momentum. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Step forward and lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the front knee tracking over the foot, not caving in; push back off the front foot to return to standing.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Suitcase Alternating Reverse Lunge', 'Using a kettlebell, held down at your side like a suitcase, a staggered-stance lunge that steps backward into a split position -- generally easier to control than a forward lunge and gentler on the front knee. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Step straight back and lower under control until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the torso tall and the front knee tracking over the foot; drive through the front heel to return to standing.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Suitcase Contralateral Curtsy Lunge', 'Using a kettlebell, held down at your side like a suitcase, a rotational lunge that steps one leg diagonally behind the other -- targets the glutes (especially glute medius) through a diagonal plane. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Step one leg diagonally behind and across the body, lowering the back knee toward the floor; keep the front knee tracking over the toes and the torso upright; push through the front leg to return to standing.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Suitcase Contralateral Forward Lunge', 'Using a kettlebell, held down at your side like a suitcase, a staggered-stance lunge that steps forward into a split position -- builds single-leg strength and challenges balance through momentum. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Step forward and lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the front knee tracking over the foot, not caving in; push back off the front foot to return to standing.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Suitcase Contralateral Knee Over Toe Split Squat', 'Using a kettlebell, held down at your side like a suitcase, a split squat variation that deliberately lets the front knee travel forward past the toes -- builds knee and ankle resilience through a deeper range. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Take a shorter stance than a normal split squat; let the front knee travel forward over and past the toes as you lower, keeping the heel down; control the descent and drive back up through the front leg.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Suitcase Contralateral Lateral Lunge', 'Using a kettlebell, held down at your side like a suitcase, a frontal-plane lunge that steps out to the side while the trailing leg stays straight -- builds hip and adductor strength outside the usual forward/back plane. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Step out to the side and sit the hips back and down over the working leg; keep the trailing leg straight with the foot flat; keep the working knee tracking over the toes; push back through the working leg to return to center.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Suitcase Contralateral Reverse Lunge', 'Using a kettlebell, held down at your side like a suitcase, a staggered-stance lunge that steps backward into a split position -- generally easier to control than a forward lunge and gentler on the front knee. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Step straight back and lower under control until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the torso tall and the front knee tracking over the foot; drive through the front heel to return to standing.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Suitcase Contralateral Russian Step Up', 'Using a kettlebell, held down at your side like a suitcase, an explosive step-up that drives the trailing leg up and forward off the box rather than just standing tall -- builds single-leg power. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Drive hard through the working leg on the box to bring the trailing knee up and slightly forward explosively; control the landing back down and reset before the next rep.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Suitcase Contralateral Split Squat', 'Using a kettlebell, held down at your side like a suitcase, a stationary staggered-stance lower-body exercise -- builds single-leg strength and balance without the stepping motion of a lunge. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Take a split stance long enough that the front knee stays over the ankle at the bottom; lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; drive through the front foot to stand.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Suitcase Contralateral Step Up', 'Using a kettlebell, held down at your side like a suitcase, a single-leg strength staple that steps fully onto an elevated surface -- builds unilateral leg strength and stability. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Place the whole foot on the box; drive through that heel to stand fully upright without pushing off the trailing leg; control the descent back down.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Suitcase Cyclist Squat', 'Using a kettlebell, held down at your side like a suitcase, a narrow-stance, heels-elevated squat that emphasizes the quads by allowing a very upright torso and deep knee bend. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Heels elevated (plate, wedge, or shoes), feet close together; sit straight down keeping the torso upright; let the knees travel forward over the toes; drive through the front of the foot to stand.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Suitcase Deadlift', 'Using a kettlebell, held down at your side like a suitcase, a deadlift performed with the load at one or both sides of the body (like picking up a suitcase) -- adds an anti-lateral-flexion core demand. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Stand the load beside the feet; hinge the hips back with a flat back to grip it; drive through the floor to stand tall while resisting leaning toward the load; lower back under control.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Suitcase Foot Elevated Contralateral Knee Over Toe Split Squat', 'Using a kettlebell, held down at your side like a suitcase, a split squat variation that deliberately lets the front knee travel forward past the toes -- builds knee and ankle resilience through a deeper range. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Take a shorter stance than a normal split squat; let the front knee travel forward over and past the toes as you lower, keeping the heel down; control the descent and drive back up through the front leg.'),
