  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Suitcase Ipsilateral Split Squat', 'Using a kettlebell, held down at your side like a suitcase, a stationary staggered-stance lower-body exercise -- builds single-leg strength and balance without the stepping motion of a lunge. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Take a split stance long enough that the front knee stays over the ankle at the bottom; lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; drive through the front foot to stand.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Suitcase Ipsilateral Step Up', 'Using a kettlebell, held down at your side like a suitcase, a single-leg strength staple that steps fully onto an elevated surface -- builds unilateral leg strength and stability. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Place the whole foot on the box; drive through that heel to stand fully upright without pushing off the trailing leg; control the descent back down.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Suitcase Walking Lunge', 'Using a kettlebell, held down at your side like a suitcase, a lunge performed continuously, stepping forward from one rep into the next -- adds a locomotion and balance demand to the standard lunge. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Step forward and lower until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; drive through the front leg to bring the back foot through into the next step, keeping the torso tall throughout.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Sumo Deadlift', 'Using a kettlebell, a wide-stance deadlift that shifts some of the work onto the inner thighs and shortens the range of motion for the back. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Wide stance with toes turned out, hands inside the knees; keep the chest up and back flat as you push the floor away; drive the hips forward to lock out at the top.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Swing', 'Using a kettlebell, a ballistic hip-hinge exercise that builds explosive hip power -- the hips do the work, not the arms or back. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Hike the weight back like a hinge, keeping the back flat; snap the hips forward explosively to swing it up to roughly chest height; let momentum bring it back down and absorb it into the next hinge.'),
  ('Single Arm Kettlebell Tall Kneeling Around the World', 'Using a kettlebell, from a tall-kneeling position, a kettlebell flow drill that circles the bell around the body at the waist -- builds grip, shoulder stability, and core control through a moving target. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Pass the bell around the waist from hand to hand, keeping the core braced and the torso still; keep the pace controlled and even in both directions.'),
  ('Single Arm Landmine Front Rack Contralateral Split Squat', 'Using a landmine-anchored bar, held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a stationary staggered-stance lower-body exercise -- builds single-leg strength and balance without the stepping motion of a lunge. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Take a split stance long enough that the front knee stays over the ankle at the bottom; lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; drive through the front foot to stand.'),
  ('Single Arm Landmine Front Rack Ipsilateral Split Squat', 'Using a landmine-anchored bar, held in the front-rack position at the shoulders, a stationary staggered-stance lower-body exercise -- builds single-leg strength and balance without the stepping motion of a lunge. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Take a split stance long enough that the front knee stays over the ankle at the bottom; lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; drive through the front foot to stand.'),
  ('Single Arm Landmine Meadows Row', 'Using a landmine-anchored bar, a single-arm landmine row performed from a side-on stance -- allows a long range of motion and strong lat contraction. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Stand side-on to the landmine, hinge forward with a flat back; row the handle up toward the hip, driving the elbow back; lower under control through a full stretch.'),
  ('Single Arm Landmine Row', 'Using a landmine-anchored bar, a pulling exercise that draws the load toward the torso -- builds the back, rear shoulders, and biceps. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Set the shoulder blades back and down before pulling; drive the elbow(s) back to bring the load to the torso, squeezing the back at the top; control the return through a full stretch.'),
  ('Single Arm Landmine Suitcase Contralateral Split Squat', 'Using a landmine-anchored bar, held down at your side like a suitcase, a stationary staggered-stance lower-body exercise -- builds single-leg strength and balance without the stepping motion of a lunge. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Take a split stance long enough that the front knee stays over the ankle at the bottom; lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; drive through the front foot to stand.'),
  ('Single Arm Macebell Order Alternating Cossack Squat', 'Using a macebell, a wide-stance lateral squat that shifts weight fully onto one leg at a time -- builds hip and ankle mobility along with single-leg strength. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Let the macebell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Take a wide stance, toes turned slightly out; sit the hips back and down over one leg while the other leg stays straight with the foot flat or heel lifted; keep the chest tall and the loaded knee tracking over the toes.'),
  ('Single Arm Macebell Order Alternating Curtsy Lunge', 'Using a macebell, a rotational lunge that steps one leg diagonally behind the other -- targets the glutes (especially glute medius) through a diagonal plane. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Let the macebell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step one leg diagonally behind and across the body, lowering the back knee toward the floor; keep the front knee tracking over the toes and the torso upright; push through the front leg to return to standing.'),
  ('Single Arm Macebell Order Alternating Forward Lunge', 'Using a macebell, a staggered-stance lunge that steps forward into a split position -- builds single-leg strength and challenges balance through momentum. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Let the macebell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step forward and lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the front knee tracking over the foot, not caving in; push back off the front foot to return to standing.'),
  ('Single Arm Macebell Order Alternating Lateral Lunge', 'Using a macebell, a frontal-plane lunge that steps out to the side while the trailing leg stays straight -- builds hip and adductor strength outside the usual forward/back plane. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Let the macebell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step out to the side and sit the hips back and down over the working leg; keep the trailing leg straight with the foot flat; keep the working knee tracking over the toes; push back through the working leg to return to center.'),
