  ('Single Arm Macebell Order Contralateral Lateral Lunge', 'Using a macebell, a frontal-plane lunge that steps out to the side while the trailing leg stays straight -- builds hip and adductor strength outside the usual forward/back plane. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Let the macebell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step out to the side and sit the hips back and down over the working leg; keep the trailing leg straight with the foot flat; keep the working knee tracking over the toes; push back through the working leg to return to center.'),
  ('Single Arm Macebell Order Contralateral Reverse Lunge', 'Using a macebell, a staggered-stance lunge that steps backward into a split position -- generally easier to control than a forward lunge and gentler on the front knee. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Let the macebell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step straight back and lower under control until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the torso tall and the front knee tracking over the foot; drive through the front heel to return to standing.'),
  ('Single Arm Macebell Order Contralateral Russian Step Up', 'Using a macebell, an explosive step-up that drives the trailing leg up and forward off the box rather than just standing tall -- builds single-leg power. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Let the macebell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Drive hard through the working leg on the box to bring the trailing knee up and slightly forward explosively; control the landing back down and reset before the next rep.'),
  ('Single Arm Macebell Order Contralateral Split Squat', 'Using a macebell, a stationary staggered-stance lower-body exercise -- builds single-leg strength and balance without the stepping motion of a lunge. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Let the macebell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Take a split stance long enough that the front knee stays over the ankle at the bottom; lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; drive through the front foot to stand.'),
  ('Single Arm Macebell Order Contralateral Step Up', 'Using a macebell, a single-leg strength staple that steps fully onto an elevated surface -- builds unilateral leg strength and stability. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Let the macebell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Place the whole foot on the box; drive through that heel to stand fully upright without pushing off the trailing leg; control the descent back down.'),
  ('Single Arm Macebell Order Cyclist Squat', 'Using a macebell, a narrow-stance, heels-elevated squat that emphasizes the quads by allowing a very upright torso and deep knee bend. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Let the macebell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Heels elevated (plate, wedge, or shoes), feet close together; sit straight down keeping the torso upright; let the knees travel forward over the toes; drive through the front of the foot to stand.'),
  ('Single Arm Macebell Order Foot Elevated Contralateral Knee Over Toe Split Squat', 'Using a macebell, with the rear/working foot elevated to increase the range and demand, a split squat variation that deliberately lets the front knee travel forward past the toes -- builds knee and ankle resilience through a deeper range. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Let the macebell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Take a shorter stance than a normal split squat; let the front knee travel forward over and past the toes as you lower, keeping the heel down; control the descent and drive back up through the front leg.'),
  ('Single Arm Macebell Order Foot Elevated Ipsilateral Knee Over Toe Split Squat', 'Using a macebell, with the rear/working foot elevated to increase the range and demand, a split squat variation that deliberately lets the front knee travel forward past the toes -- builds knee and ankle resilience through a deeper range. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Let the macebell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Take a shorter stance than a normal split squat; let the front knee travel forward over and past the toes as you lower, keeping the heel down; control the descent and drive back up through the front leg.'),
  ('Single Arm Macebell Order Ipsilateral Cossack Squat', 'Using a macebell, a wide-stance lateral squat that shifts weight fully onto one leg at a time -- builds hip and ankle mobility along with single-leg strength. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Let the macebell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Take a wide stance, toes turned slightly out; sit the hips back and down over one leg while the other leg stays straight with the foot flat or heel lifted; keep the chest tall and the loaded knee tracking over the toes.'),
  ('Single Arm Macebell Order Ipsilateral Curtsy Lunge', 'Using a macebell, a rotational lunge that steps one leg diagonally behind the other -- targets the glutes (especially glute medius) through a diagonal plane. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Let the macebell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step one leg diagonally behind and across the body, lowering the back knee toward the floor; keep the front knee tracking over the toes and the torso upright; push through the front leg to return to standing.'),
  ('Single Arm Macebell Order Ipsilateral Forward Lunge', 'Using a macebell, a staggered-stance lunge that steps forward into a split position -- builds single-leg strength and challenges balance through momentum. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Let the macebell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step forward and lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the front knee tracking over the foot, not caving in; push back off the front foot to return to standing.'),
  ('Single Arm Macebell Order Ipsilateral Knee Over Toe Split Squat', 'Using a macebell, a split squat variation that deliberately lets the front knee travel forward past the toes -- builds knee and ankle resilience through a deeper range. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Let the macebell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Take a shorter stance than a normal split squat; let the front knee travel forward over and past the toes as you lower, keeping the heel down; control the descent and drive back up through the front leg.'),
  ('Single Arm Macebell Order Ipsilateral Lateral Lunge', 'Using a macebell, a frontal-plane lunge that steps out to the side while the trailing leg stays straight -- builds hip and adductor strength outside the usual forward/back plane. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Let the macebell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step out to the side and sit the hips back and down over the working leg; keep the trailing leg straight with the foot flat; keep the working knee tracking over the toes; push back through the working leg to return to center.'),
  ('Single Arm Macebell Order Ipsilateral Reverse Lunge', 'Using a macebell, a staggered-stance lunge that steps backward into a split position -- generally easier to control than a forward lunge and gentler on the front knee. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Let the macebell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step straight back and lower under control until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the torso tall and the front knee tracking over the foot; drive through the front heel to return to standing.'),
  ('Single Arm Macebell Order Ipsilateral Russian Step Up', 'Using a macebell, an explosive step-up that drives the trailing leg up and forward off the box rather than just standing tall -- builds single-leg power. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Let the macebell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Drive hard through the working leg on the box to bring the trailing knee up and slightly forward explosively; control the landing back down and reset before the next rep.'),
  ('Single Arm Macebell Order Ipsilateral Split Squat', 'Using a macebell, a stationary staggered-stance lower-body exercise -- builds single-leg strength and balance without the stepping motion of a lunge. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Let the macebell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Take a split stance long enough that the front knee stays over the ankle at the bottom; lower straight down until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; drive through the front foot to stand.'),
  ('Single Arm Macebell Order Ipsilateral Step Up', 'Using a macebell, a single-leg strength staple that steps fully onto an elevated surface -- builds unilateral leg strength and stability. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Let the macebell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Place the whole foot on the box; drive through that heel to stand fully upright without pushing off the trailing leg; control the descent back down.'),
  ('Single Arm Macebell Order Squat', 'Using a macebell, a squat performed holding the clubbell/macebell in the "order" position (upright at the shoulder) -- adds an asymmetric core-stability demand on top of the squat. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Let the macebell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Keep the implement stacked vertically at the shoulder without letting it drift forward or out to the side; sit the hips back and down keeping the torso tall; brace the core to resist the pull of the offset load.'),
  ('Single Arm Macebell Order Walking Lunge', 'Using a macebell, a lunge performed continuously, stepping forward from one rep into the next -- adds a locomotion and balance demand to the standard lunge. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Let the macebell''s offset weight swing naturally through its arc rather than muscling it -- control comes from timing, not brute force. Step forward and lower until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; drive through the front leg to bring the back foot through into the next step, keeping the torso tall throughout.'),
  ('Single Arm Resistance Band Low Row', 'Using a resistance band, a band-resisted seated-style row pulled from a low angle -- targets the mid-back with continuous tension. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Sit or stand tall, arms extended toward the anchor; row the handles to the ribs by driving the elbows straight back and squeezing the shoulder blades together; return under control.'),
  ('Single Arm Resistance Band Mid Row', 'Using a resistance band, a band-resisted row pulled from chest height -- targets the mid-back and rear shoulders with continuous tension. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Stand tall facing the anchor, arms extended; row the handles to the ribs by driving the elbows back and squeezing the shoulder blades together; return under control.'),
  ('Single Arm Suspension Alternating Cossack Squat', 'Using a suspension trainer, a wide-stance lateral squat that shifts weight fully onto one leg at a time -- builds hip and ankle mobility along with single-leg strength. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Take a wide stance, toes turned slightly out; sit the hips back and down over one leg while the other leg stays straight with the foot flat or heel lifted; keep the chest tall and the loaded knee tracking over the toes.'),
  ('Single Arm Suspension Contralateral Cossack Squat', 'Using a suspension trainer, a wide-stance lateral squat that shifts weight fully onto one leg at a time -- builds hip and ankle mobility along with single-leg strength. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Take a wide stance, toes turned slightly out; sit the hips back and down over one leg while the other leg stays straight with the foot flat or heel lifted; keep the chest tall and the loaded knee tracking over the toes.'),
  ('Single Arm Suspension Cyclist Squat', 'Using a suspension trainer, a narrow-stance, heels-elevated squat that emphasizes the quads by allowing a very upright torso and deep knee bend. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Heels elevated (plate, wedge, or shoes), feet close together; sit straight down keeping the torso upright; let the knees travel forward over the toes; drive through the front of the foot to stand.'),
  ('Single Arm Suspension Foot Elevated Contralateral Cossack Squat', 'Using a suspension trainer, with the rear/working foot elevated to increase the range and demand, a wide-stance lateral squat that shifts weight fully onto one leg at a time -- builds hip and ankle mobility along with single-leg strength. The load is on the opposite side from the working leg, which raises the anti-rotation/anti-lateral-flexion demand on the core.', 'Take a wide stance, toes turned slightly out; sit the hips back and down over one leg while the other leg stays straight with the foot flat or heel lifted; keep the chest tall and the loaded knee tracking over the toes.'),
  ('Single Arm Suspension Foot Elevated Ipsilateral Cossack Squat', 'Using a suspension trainer, with the rear/working foot elevated to increase the range and demand, a wide-stance lateral squat that shifts weight fully onto one leg at a time -- builds hip and ankle mobility along with single-leg strength. The load is on the same side as the working leg, which is generally more stable than loading the opposite side.', 'Take a wide stance, toes turned slightly out; sit the hips back and down over one leg while the other leg stays straight with the foot flat or heel lifted; keep the chest tall and the loaded knee tracking over the toes.'),
