  ('Single Arm Suspension Low Row', 'Using a suspension trainer, a band-resisted seated-style row pulled from a low angle -- targets the mid-back with continuous tension. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Sit or stand tall, arms extended toward the anchor; row the handles to the ribs by driving the elbows straight back and squeezing the shoulder blades together; return under control.'),
  ('Single Arm Suspension Low Switch Cossack Squat', 'Using a suspension trainer, a wide-stance lateral squat that shifts weight fully onto one leg at a time -- builds hip and ankle mobility along with single-leg strength. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Take a wide stance, toes turned slightly out; sit the hips back and down over one leg while the other leg stays straight with the foot flat or heel lifted; keep the chest tall and the loaded knee tracking over the toes.'),
  ('Single Arm Suspension Mid Row', 'Using a suspension trainer, a band-resisted row pulled from chest height -- targets the mid-back and rear shoulders with continuous tension. Working one side at a time adds an anti-rotation core demand on top of the base movement.', 'Stand tall facing the anchor, arms extended; row the handles to the ribs by driving the elbows back and squeezing the shoulder blades together; return under control.'),
  ('Slam Ball Otis Up', 'Using a slam ball, a get-up-style flow move that presses the weight overhead from lying and rolls up to standing (or a chosen partial range) while keeping the arm locked out -- builds shoulder stability and total-body coordination.', 'Press the weight overhead first and keep the arm locked out through the whole movement; roll and stand up (or move through the chosen segment) without letting the weight drift from directly over the shoulder; reverse under control.'),
  ('Slam Ball Overhead Slam', 'Using a slam ball, a full-body power exercise that drives a weighted implement from overhead into the ground.', 'Raise the implement fully overhead; drive it down forcefully into the ground in front of you, using the hips and core, not just the arms; catch the bounce (if any) and reset.'),
  ('Slam Ball Russian Twist', 'Using a slam ball, a rotational core exercise performed in a seated, torso-leaned-back position -- targets the obliques through trunk rotation.', 'Lean back to a stable torso angle, feet lifted or grounded; rotate the torso (and load, if used) from side to side under control, keeping the rotation coming from the trunk, not just the arms.'),
  ('Slam Ball Tall Kneeling Overhead Slam', 'Using a slam ball, from a tall-kneeling position, a full-body power exercise that drives a weighted implement from overhead into the ground.', 'Raise the implement fully overhead; drive it down forcefully into the ground in front of you, using the hips and core, not just the arms; catch the bounce (if any) and reset.'),
  ('Slider Body Saw', 'Using sliders (or a towel/paper plate on a smooth floor), a plank variation that slides the body forward and back through the shoulders -- adds an anti-extension challenge on top of the static plank.', 'Set up in a forearm plank with feet on sliders (or rings); push the body back and pull forward through the shoulders while keeping the hips level and core braced throughout.'),
  ('Slider Bulgarian Bag Back Rack Curtsy Lunge', 'Using a Bulgarian bag, held across the upper back, a rotational lunge that steps one leg diagonally behind the other -- targets the glutes (especially glute medius) through a diagonal plane.', 'Step one leg diagonally behind and across the body, lowering the back knee toward the floor; keep the front knee tracking over the toes and the torso upright; push through the front leg to return to standing.'),
  ('Slider Bulgarian Bag Back Rack Lateral Lunge', 'Using a Bulgarian bag, held across the upper back, a frontal-plane lunge that steps out to the side while the trailing leg stays straight -- builds hip and adductor strength outside the usual forward/back plane.', 'Step out to the side and sit the hips back and down over the working leg; keep the trailing leg straight with the foot flat; keep the working knee tracking over the toes; push back through the working leg to return to center.'),
  ('Slider Bulgarian Bag Back Rack Reverse Lunge', 'Using a Bulgarian bag, held across the upper back, a staggered-stance lunge that steps backward into a split position -- generally easier to control than a forward lunge and gentler on the front knee.', 'Step straight back and lower under control until both knees reach roughly 90 degrees; keep the torso tall and the front knee tracking over the foot; drive through the front heel to return to standing.'),
